{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba experiments/zkir-k/tools/divergence_tests.py; nl -ba wiki/zkir/zkir-k-definition.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"Milestone 5: the divergence findings as executable tests (oracle 2).\n     2\t\n     3\tEach case is a small ZKIR v3 program written here, a preimage, and the expected\n     4\tpattern: the off-circuit status on both sides (K witness and Rust\n     5\t`preprocess`), and the outcome the constraint checker must assign to one\n     6\tspecific gate. Findings refer to arc-zkir docs/zkir-v3-divergence-review.md at\n     7\tmidnight-ledger 92e8bdd3 (wiki/zkir/zkir-v3-divergence-review.md); \"K1\" is a\n     8\tcandidate found while writing the K semantics.\n     9\t\n    10\tUsage: uv run --group zkir-k python experiments/zkir-k/tools/divergence_tests.py\n    11\tWrites the programs to corpus/divergence/ and exits 0 iff every case matches.\n    12\t\"\"\"\n    13\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n    14\t\n    15\timport json\n    16\timport subprocess\n    17\timport sys\n    18\timport tempfile\n    19\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    20\t\n    21\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    22\timport zkir_kast  # noqa: E402\n    23\timport zkir_values as zv  # noqa: E402\n    24\tfrom zkir_run import Runner  # noqa: E402\n    25\t\n    26\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    27\tOUT = HERE.parent / 'corpus' / 'divergence'\n    28\tORACLE = Path.home() / 'Moriarty/repos/_build/ledger-92e8bdd3/target/release/zkir-oracle'\n    29\t\n    30\tNATIVE = 'Scalar<BLS12-381>'\n    31\t\n    32\t\n    33\tdef program(inputs, instructions, outputs=(), comm=False):\n    34\t    return {'version': {'major': 3, 'minor': 0},\n    35\t            'inputs': [{'name': n, 'type': t} for n, t in inputs],\n    36\t            'outputs': list(outputs), 'do_communications_commitment': comm,\n    37\t            'instructions': instructions}\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\tdef hexle(n: int) -> str:\n    41\t    b = n.to_bytes((n.bit_length() + 7) // 8 or 1, 'little')\n    42\t    return '0x' + b.hex()\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\t# --- cases -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    46\t# (name, finding, program, raw inputs, expected K status, expected Rust status,\n    47\t#  gate substring, expected outcome kind, note)\n    48\tG8 = zv.JUBJUB_G\n    49\tCASES = []\n    50\t\n    51\tCASES.append(('f02_assert_non_boolean', 'Finding 2',\n    52\t    program([('%c', NATIVE)], [{'op': 'assert', 'cond': '%c'}]), [2],\n    53\t    'error', 'error', 'assert', 'holds',\n    54\t    'off-circuit demands cond == 1, the circuit only cond != 0'))\n    55\t\n    56\tCASES.append(('f03_guard_uncoupled', 'Finding 3',\n    57\t    program([('%g', NATIVE)], [\n    58\t        {'op': 'public_input', 'guard': '%g', 'type': NATIVE, 'output': '%x'},\n    59\t        {'op': 'add', 'a': '%x', 'b': '0x01', 'output': '%y'}]), [0],\n    60\t    'ok', 'ok', 'public_input', 'holds',\n    61\t    'an inactive guard yields the default value off-circuit; the gate never mentions the guard'))\n    62\t\n    63\tCASES.append(('f04_less_than_odd_bits', 'Finding 4',\n    64\t    program([('%a', NATIVE), ('%b', NATIVE)], [\n    65\t        {'op': 'less_than', 'a': '%a', 'b': '%b', 'bits': 3, 'output': '%lt'}]), [8, 9],\n    66\t    'error', 'error', 'less_than', 'unknown',\n    67\t    'a = 8 exceeds 3 bits off-circuit; the chip would accept the padded 4-bit bound (output absent, so unknown)'))\n    68\t\n    69\tCASES.append(('f05_noncanonical_foreign_limbs', 'Finding 5',\n    70\t    program([('%x', 'Base<Secp256k1>')], [\n    71\t        {'op': 'add', 'a': '%x', 'b': '%x', 'output': '%y'}]),\n    72\t    [((zv.K256P + 5 - 1) & ((1 << 192) - 1)), ((zv.K256P + 5 - 1) >> 192)],\n    73\t    'ok', 'ok', 'add', 'holds',\n    74\t    'limbs encoding p + 5 decode (reduced) as 5 on both sides'))\n    75\t\n    76\tCASES.append(('f06_cond_select_bytes32', 'Finding 6',\n    77\t    program([('%bit', NATIVE), ('%a', 'Bytes<32>'), ('%b', 'Bytes<32>')], [\n    78\t        {'op': 'cond_select', 'bit': '%bit', 'a': '%a', 'b': '%b', 'output': '%o'}]),\n    79\t    [1, 12345, 7, 999, 0],\n    80\t    'ok', 'ok', 'cond_select', 'synthErr',\n    81\t    'cond_select on Bytes32 is accepted off-circuit but has no in-circuit arm'))\n    82\t\n    83\tCASES.append(('f07_constrain_eq_jubjub_scalar', 'Finding 7',\n    84\t    program([('%s', 'Scalar<Jubjub>'), ('%t', 'Scalar<Jubjub>')], [\n    85\t        {'op': 'constrain_eq', 'a': '%s', 'b': '%t'}]), [42, 42],\n    86\t    'ok', 'ok', 'constrain_eq', 'synthErr',\n    87\t    'constrain_eq on JubjubScalar is accepted off-circuit but has no in-circuit arm'))\n    88\t\n    89\tCASES.append(('f08_bytes32_from_low_high_foreign_high', 'Finding 8',\n    90\t    program([('%lo', NATIVE), ('%hi', 'Base<Secp256k1>')], [\n    91\t        {'op': 'bytes32_from_low_high', 'inputs': ['%lo', '%hi'], 'output': '%b'}]),\n    92\t    [12345] + zv.enc_foreign(200, zv.K256P, 64, 4),\n    93\t    'ok', 'ok', 'bytes32_from_low_high', 'synthErr',\n    94\t    'a foreign high operand is accepted off-circuit, the circuit requires Native'))\n    95\t\n    96\tCASES.append(('f10_reconstitute_bits_256', 'Finding 10',\n    97\t    program([('%d', NATIVE), ('%m', NATIVE)], [\n    98\t        {'op': 'reconstitute_field', 'divisor': '%d', 'modulus': '%m', 'bits': 256, 'output': '%o'}]), [1, 1],\n    99\t    'wfError', 'error', 'reconstitute_field', 'n/a',\n   100\t    'bits >= 256 is rejected statically (Rust off-circuit: Excessive bit count; keygen would underflow)'))\n   101\t\n   102\t# a Curve25519 point on the curve but outside the prime-order subgroup: (x, -y) of a torsion\n   103\t# point... simplest: the order-2 point (0, -1)\n   104\tCASES.append(('f11_curve25519_torsion_point', 'Finding 11',\n   105\t    program([('%x', 'Base<Curve25519>'), ('%y', 'Base<Curve25519>')], [\n   106\t        {'op': 'from_coordinates', 'inputs': ['%x', '%y'], 'output': '%p'}]),\n   107\t    zv.enc_foreign(0, zv.C25519P, 64, 4) + zv.enc_foreign(zv.C25519P - 1, zv.C25519P, 64, 4),\n   108\t    'error', 'error', 'from_coordinates', 'violated',\n   109\t    '(0, -1) has order 2: on the curve, not in the subgroup; off-circuit both sides error, in circuit the cofactor-cleared point is unsatisfiable'))\n   110\t\n   111\tCASES.append(('f12_ec_mul_generator_p256', 'Finding 12',\n   112\t    program([('%s', 'Scalar<Secp256r1>')], [\n   113\t        {'op': 'ec_mul_generator', 'scalar': '%s', 'output': '%p'}]),\n   114\t    zv.enc_foreign(7, zv.P256N, 64, 4),\n   115\t    'error', 'error', 'ec_mul_generator', 'synthErr',\n   116\t    'no generator for secp256r1 scalars on either side'))\n   117\t\n   118\tCASES.append(('f13_chip_gating_from_bytes32', 'Finding 13',\n   119\t    program([('%b', 'Bytes<32>')], [\n   120\t        {'op': 'from_bytes32', 'bytes': '%b', 'type': 'Base<Secp256k1>', 'output': '%x'},\n   121\t        {'op': 'add', 'a': '%x', 'b': '%x', 'output': '%y'}]), [12345, 0],\n   122\t    'ok', 'ok', 'from_bytes32', 'synthErr',\n   123\t    'the secp256k1 chip is never initialised (used_chips ignores from_bytes32)'))\n   124\t\n   125\twrong_x = (G8[0] + 2) % zv.R\n   126\tCASES.append(('k01_jubjub_from_coordinates_parity_only', 'K1 (new)',\n   127\t    program([('%x', NATIVE), ('%y', NATIVE)], [\n   128\t        {'op': 'from_coordinates', 'inputs': ['%x', '%y'], 'output': '%p'},\n   129\t        {'op': 'into_coordinates', 'point': '%p', 'outputs': ['%px', '%py']}]), [wrong_x, G8[1]],\n   130\t    'ok', 'ok', 'from_coordinates', 'violated',\n   131\t    'off-circuit only the parity of x is used (decompression), so a wrong x yields the real point; in-circuit (x, y) must lie on the curve'))\n   132\t\n   133\tCASES.append(('k02_transcript_too_short_panics', 'K2 (new)',\n   134\t    program([('%a', NATIVE)], [\n   135\t        {'op': 'private_input', 'guard': None, 'type': NATIVE, 'output': '%x'},\n   136\t        {'op': 'add', 'a': '%a', 'b': '%x', 'output': '%y'}]), [1],\n   137\t    'error', 'oracle-failed', 'private_input', 'unknown',\n   138\t    'an unguarded private_input with an empty private transcript: the crate indexes the slice and panics (index out of range) instead of returning an error; K reports an error'))\n   139\t\n   140\tCASES.append(('f01_reconstitute_overflow', 'Finding 1 (retired)',\n   141\t    program([('%d', NATIVE), ('%m', NATIVE)], [\n   142\t        {'op': 'reconstitute_field', 'divisor': '%d', 'modulus': '%m', 'bits': 8, 'output': '%o'}]),\n   143\t    [(zv.R - 1) >> 8, 255],\n   144\t    'error', 'error', 'reconstitute_field', 'unknown',\n   145\t    'divisor * 2^8 + modulus >= r: off-circuit overflow error; in circuit the sum would wrap mod r'))\n   146\t\n   147\t\n   148\tdef oracle(path: Path, pre: dict) -> dict:\n   149\t    with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile('w', suffix='.json', delete=False) as f:\n   150\t        json.dump(pre, f)\n   151\t    res = subprocess.run([str(ORACLE), str(path), f.name], capture_output=True, text=True)\n   152\t    return json.loads(res.stdout) if res.returncode == 0 else {'status': 'oracle-failed', 'error': res.stderr[-300:]}\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\tdef main() -> int:\n   156\t    OUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n   157\t    runner = Runner()\n   158\t    check_runner = Runner(HERE.parent / 'semantics' / 'zkir-check-kompiled')\n   159\t    failures = 0\n   160\t    for name, finding, prog, raw, exp_k, exp_r, gate_sub, exp_outcome, note in CASES:\n   161\t        path = OUT / f'{name}.zkir'\n   162\t        path.write_text(json.dumps(prog, indent=1) + '\\n')\n   163\t        pre = {'inputs': [str(x) for x in raw], 'binding_input': '42'}\n   164\t        # static well-formedness first (some findings are rejected there)\n   165\t        from pyk.kast.inner import KSort\n   166\t        from pyk.kore.parser import KoreParser\n   167\t        term = zkir_kast.load_program(path)\n   168\t        res = check_runner.krun.run_process(check_runner.krun.kast_to_kore(term, KSort('Program')))\n   169\t        wf = zkir_kast.wf_result(check_runner.krun.pretty_print(check_runner.krun.kore_to_kast(KoreParser(res.stdout).pattern())))\n   170\t        r = oracle(path, pre)\n   171\t        if wf != 'wfOk':\n   172\t            k_status = 'wfError'\n   173\t            outcome = 'n/a'\n   174\t            detail = wf\n   175\t        else:\n   176\t            k = runner.run(term, pre)\n   177\t            k_status = k['status']\n   178\t            norm = lambda t: t.replace('_', '').lower()\n   179\t            hits = [v for v in k['violations'] if norm(gate_sub) in norm(v[2])]\n   180\t            if hits:\n   181\t                outcome = hits[0][0]\n   182\t                detail = f'{hits[0][0]}: {hits[0][1]}'\n   183\t            else:\n   184\t                outcome = 'holds' if any(gate_sub in g for g in [gate_sub]) else 'holds'\n   185\t                detail = k.get('error', 'ok')\n   186\t        ok = (k_status == exp_k) and (r['status'] == exp_r) and (outcome == exp_outcome)\n   187\t        failures += not ok\n   188\t        print(f\"{'PASS' if ok else 'FAIL'}  {name:42} {finding:20} K={k_status:8} Rust={r['status']:6} gate={gate_sub}:{outcome}\")\n   189\t        print(f\"        {note}\")\n   190\t        print(f\"        K: {detail[:100]} | Rust: {r.get('error', 'ok')[:100]}\")\n   191\t        if not ok:\n   192\t            print(f\"        expected K={exp_k} Rust={exp_r} outcome={exp_outcome}\")\n   193\t    print(f'\\n{len(CASES) - failures}/{len(CASES)} divergence cases behave as expected')\n   194\t    return 1 if failures else 0\n   195\t\n   196\t\n   197\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   198\t    sys.exit(main())\n     1\t---\n     2\tid: zkir.k-definition\n     3\ttype: semantics\n     4\ttitle: The ZKIR semantics in K, as built\n     5\tstatus: active\n     6\tupdated_at: 2026-09-05T21:10:00Z\n     7\tsources:\n     8\t  - SRC-0023\n     9\t  - SRC-0025\n    10\t  - SRC-0006\n    11\t---\n    12\t\n    13\t# The ZKIR semantics in K, as built\n    14\t\n    15\tThis page records what the executable K definition of ZKIR v3 under `experiments/zkir-k/` is, how it was checked, and what the checks found. The decisions it implements are on [../zkir-k-semantics-plan.md](../zkir-k-semantics-plan.md); the instruction set, types and VM it formalises are described on [zkir-instruction-set.md](zkir-instruction-set.md), [zkir-type-system.md](zkir-type-system.md) and [zkir-vm-semantics.md](zkir-vm-semantics.md). Everything here was reproduced on 2026-09-05 with K v7.1.337 and the receipts named in each claim.\n    16\t\n    17\t## Layout\n    18\t\n    19\tThe definition is one K definition split into modules, each with the Rust file it follows in its header (CLM-0731; experiments/zkir-k/semantics; repository observation; reproduced; high; S1):\n    20\t\n    21\t| Module | File | Follows | Content |\n    22\t|---|---|---|---|\n    23\t| `ZKIR-SYNTAX`, `ZKIR-WF` | `zkir-syntax.k` | `ir.rs`, `ir_types.rs` | 34 instructions, 13 types, operands, guards, alignments; `reads`, `writes`, `encodedLen`; static well-formedness |\n    24\t| `ZKIR-FIELD` | `zkir-field.k` | `transient-crypto/src/curve.rs`, midnight-curves 0.3.1 | eight moduli, modular arithmetic on K `Int`, Tonelli-Shanks square roots |\n    25\t| `ZKIR-CURVES` | `zkir-curves.k` | midnight-curves 0.3.1, midnight-circuits 7.2.4 `ecc/curves.rs` | affine twisted-Edwards (Jubjub, Curve25519) and Weierstrass (secp256k1, secp256r1) arithmetic, subgroup tests, Jubjub decompression |\n    26\t| `ZKIR-VALUES` | `zkir-values.k` | `ir_instructions/encode.rs`, midnight-circuits `Instantiable` | the 13 value constructors, `encode_offcircuit`, `from_public_input`, input decoding |\n    27\t| `ZKIR-CONSTANTS` | `zkir-constants.k` (generated) | midnight-circuits `hash/poseidon/constants/blstrs.rs`, `ecc/hash_to_curve/mtc_params.rs` | 204 Poseidon round constants, the MDS matrix, the Shallue-van de Woestijne parameters |\n    28\t| `ZKIR-HASH` | `zkir-hash.k` | midnight-circuits `poseidon_cpu.rs`, `htc_gadget.rs`, `mtc_cpu.rs`; FIPS 180-4; Keccak | Poseidon sponge (fixed and variable length), hash-to-curve, SHA-256, Keccak-256 |\n    29\t| `ZKIR-OPS` | `zkir-ops.k` | `ir_instructions/*.rs` off-circuit halves, `transient-crypto/src/{fab,repr}.rs` | one function per `*_offcircuit`, boolean and bit-bound resolution, aligned byte layout for the standard hashes |\n    30\t| `ZKIR-VM` | `zkir-vm.k` | `ir_vm.rs` `preprocess` | the configuration, sequencing, one witness rule per instruction, constraint emission, transcript and commitment checks, generation mode |\n    31\t| `ZKIR-CONSTRAINTS` | `zkir-constraints.k` | `ir_vm.rs` `circuit`, `ir_instructions/*.rs` in-circuit halves, `used_chips` | the in-circuit relation of each gate, evaluated on the final witness |\n    32\t| `ZKIR-EXT` | `zkir-ext.k`, `zkir-sha512-constants.k` | `midnight-zkir` 2ffe2d1 `zkir/src/` | Bool, Byte, Bytes<n>; and, or, xor, concat, slice, nth, reverse, load_constant, sha512 |\n    33\t\n    34\tPrograms enter through `tools/zkir_kast.py`, which reads the JSON artifact and builds the `Program` term with pyk, mirroring the serde layer of `ir.rs` (variables start with `%`, immediates are little-endian hex below r, `guard: null`, tagged alignments); the same script rejects what `IrSource::load` rejects (CLM-0732; experiments/zkir-k/tools/zkir_kast.py; repository observation; reproduced; high; S1). A run is `job(Program, Preimage)`, where the preimage mirrors `ProofPreimage`: raw inputs, binding input, optional commitment and opening, private transcript, public transcript inputs and outputs (CLM-0732).\n    35\t\n    36\t## What a run produces\n    37\t\n    38\tThe configuration carries the seven components of the VM page as cells (`<mem>`, `<pi>`, `<skips>`, `<pubInIdx>`, `<pubOutIdx>`, `<privIdx>`, `<outputs>`) plus `<constraints>`, `<status>`, `<chips>` and `<verdicts>` (CLM-0733; experiments/zkir-k/semantics/zkir-vm.k; repository observation; reproduced; high; S1). Each instruction first appends its gate to `<constraints>` and then runs its witness half; the first witness error sets `<status>` and freezes the witness cells while every later instruction still emits its gate, so the constraint set at the end is always the whole circuit's (CLM-0733). At the end, the transcripts must be fully consumed and, when the program declares a communications commitment, the commitment must equal the Poseidon commitment of the raw inputs and the encoded outputs, exactly as in `preprocess` (CLM-0733). Finally every gate is evaluated against the witness and `<verdicts>` records, per gate, whether it holds, is violated, cannot be synthesised (a missing in-circuit dispatch arm or an uninitialised chip), or is unknown because the witness stopped before the register existed (CLM-0733).\n    39\t\n    40\tGates are instruction-level relations, not PLONKish gate rows. `gate(add(a, b, o))` states that register `o` equals the sum of `a` and `b` under the type dispatch of `add_incircuit`; `gate(assert(c))` states `c` is non-zero; `piGate(i, g, x)` states that public input `i` equals `x` when the boolean `g` is 1 and 0 otherwise (CLM-0734; experiments/zkir-k/semantics/zkir-constraints.k; repository observation; reproduced; high; S1). Because the relation is evaluated on the concrete witness, a run establishes that the honest witness satisfies the emitted constraints, never that no other witness does; soundness stays with the arc-zkir theorems, as the plan says (CLM-0734).\n    41\t\n    42\t## How it was checked\n    43\t\n    44\tThree layers, each with its receipt:\n    45\t\n    46\t1. Unit checks against an independent Python implementation of the same formulas (`tools/unit_values.py`): field inverses and square roots, the non-residuosity of both Edwards `d` constants, the Jubjub subgroup generator as eight times the curve generator, scalar multiples on all four curves, the order of each generator, encode and decode round trips for every type, and the parity-only behaviour of Jubjub decompression. 41 of 41 pass (CLM-0726).\n    47\t2. Known-answer checks against the Rust crate for the hashes (`tools/unit_hash.py`): Poseidon on zero to three inputs, hash-to-curve on zero to three inputs, SHA-256 and Keccak-256 through one-, 32- and 232-byte aligned preimages, and the aligned byte layout itself. 18 of 18 pass; Poseidon and hash-to-curve matched on the first run, which also confirms that the crate's paired partial rounds are an algebraic rewrite of the plain round function (CLM-0726).\n    48\t3. Differential runs against the crate's own `preprocess` (`tools/diff_test.py`). The harness draws typed raw inputs, runs the K definition in generation mode until the transcript needs are stable, then runs K and `zkir-oracle` on the same preimage and compares status, every register's variant and encoding, the public-input vector and the skip vector. Over the 65 programs of the corpus, 314 comparisons agree and none disagree; 37 are successful witness runs, the rest compare error paths (CLM-0728). All gates hold on every successful run (CLM-0729).\n    49\t\n    50\tThe corpus is the 43 inline programs of the crate's tests at 92e8bdd3 (seeded with their literal inputs where the tests give them), the six version-3 micro-dao precompiles of midnight-zkir 2ffe2d1, the seven Moriarty escrow and swap artifacts, nine handmade programs that exercise every instruction on every type it supports, and seven handmade negatives for the static checks (CLM-0724, CLM-0728). The escrow, swap and micro-dao programs never reach a successful witness with generated inputs, because their assertions need a consistent transaction context; for them the harness compares error paths only (CLM-0728).\n    51\t\n    52\t## What the checks found\n    53\t\n    54\t- The thirteen findings of the arc-zkir divergence review reproduce as programs (`tools/divergence_tests.py`): for each, the off-circuit status agrees between K and the crate, and the gate outcome is the one the review describes, for example `assert` of 2 fails off-circuit while its gate holds, `cond_select` on Bytes32 and `constrain_eq` on JubjubScalar succeed off-circuit while their gates are synthesis errors, `bytes32_from_low_high` with a foreign `high` succeeds off-circuit while its gate is a synthesis error, `less_than` with a 3-bit bound rejects 8 off-circuit while the padded 4-bit gate would accept it, a program touching secp256k1 only through `from_bytes32` succeeds off-circuit while its gate reports the chip uninitialised, and the order-2 Curve25519 point is rejected on both sides (CLM-0730).\n    55\t- One divergence not in the review: Jubjub `from_coordinates` off-circuit goes through point decompression and uses only the parity of `x`, so `(x+2, y)` yields the true point on both K and the crate while the exact-coordinate gate is violated; the Agda trust base assumes the exact behaviour (CLM-0735; evidence/zkir-k-divergence-tests-2026-09-05.txt case `k01` and repos/input-output-hk/arc-zkir/src/zkir-v3/Assumptions.agda `fromCoordsJ-coordsJ`; executed test; reproduced; high; S1). Recorded in [../contradictions.md](../contradictions.md).\n    56\t- The Rust `preprocess` does not enforce single assignment; the K static check does, so a program that rewrites a register is rejected by K before the run and accepted by the crate (CLM-0725). No program in the corpus does this.\n    57\t- Error messages agree in substance; the only textual difference is the crate printing field elements as little-endian hex where K prints decimals (CLM-0728).\n    58\t\n    59\t## The extension surface\n    60\t\n    61\t`ZKIR-EXT` is a second main module on the same definition for the standalone `midnight-zkir` crate at 2ffe2d1: the types Bool, Byte and Bytes<n> (Bytes<32> keeps the base representation so every base rule applies), the instructions `and`, `or`, `xor` over Bool, `concat`, `slice`, `nth`, `reverse` over byte strings, `load_constant` from a typed raw encoding, `sha512`, logical `neg` on Bool, and the canonical-form check that 2ffe2d1's decoder performs by re-encoding every decoded value; `reverse_bytes` is rejected at the preprocessor as the crate removed it (CLM-0739; experiments/zkir-k/semantics/zkir-ext.k; repository observation; reproduced; high; S1). Against a second `zkir-oracle` built from that crate, 61 of its inline test programs, the six micro-dao precompiles and the handmade programs give 366 comparisons that all agree, with 40 successful runs and no non-holding gate (CLM-0737). The canonical-form check is the visible behavioural difference between the two crates: a Jubjub point input with a wrong `x` of the right parity is accepted at 92e8bdd3 and rejected at 2ffe2d1 (CLM-0737).\n    62\t\n    63\tA second new finding came out of this run (K2): both crates slice the transcript directly, so an unguarded `public_input` or `private_input` whose transcript is too short panics with an index-out-of-range rather than returning an error; K reports an error, and the divergence test `k02` reproduces the panic through the oracle (CLM-0738).\n    64\t\n    65\t## The other oracles\n    66\t\n    67\tThe arc-zkir v3 Agda development type-checks in full with its own Nix toolchain, fifteen modules under `--safe` (CLM-0736; evidence/arc-zkir-agda-typecheck-2026-09-05.txt; executed test; reproduced; high; S1). It cannot execute programs: it is parametric over an `Assumptions` record that supplies the field, curve and hash primitives, and the repository has no concrete instance of that record, so oracle 3 of the plan remains a correspondence by reading, not by running (CLM-0736; repos/input-output-hk/arc-zkir/src/zkir-v3/Assumptions.agda; repository observation; reproduced; high; S4).\n    68\t\n    69\tMidnight's `k-rust` (0.4.0, built from the pinned checkout) runs the K tutorial lesson in three seconds and parses terms of `ZKIR-SYNTAX` in under four, but neither `krust krun` on a one-instruction program nor `krust kcompile` of the full definition finished: the compile was killed after 25 minutes of CPU with no output, where canonical K compiles the same definition in twenty seconds (CLM-0740; evidence/k-rust-compatibility-2026-09-05.txt; executed test; reproduced; high; S1). The definition uses nothing k-rust documents as unsupported, so the gap is performance of its frontend pipeline on a definition of this size, not a feature boundary; running the semantics inside the Midnight TypeScript tooling stays a goal, as the plan said, not a gate (CLM-0740).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat WIKI_SCHEMA.md raw/assignments/modernizing-marlowe-assignment-2026-09-02.xml; rg -n 'zkir|ZKIR|K Framework' wiki/index.md; cat /home/charl/.agents/skills/superpowers/skills/using-superpowers/references/codex-tools.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Moriarty wiki operating schema\n\n## Purpose and layers\n\nThis repository implements Andrej Karpathy's LLM Wiki pattern for Moriarty's\nMarlowe-to-Compact/ZKIR design study.\n\n- `raw/` is immutable source material, prompt inputs, receipts, and dated\n  research notes. Corrections supersede; they do not overwrite.\n- `wiki/` is maintained synthesis. Update an existing concept page instead of\n  creating a near-duplicate.\n- `WIKI_SCHEMA.md` and `AGENTS.md` define the operating contract.\n- `evidence/` contains inventories, repository locks, deployment records, and\n  experiment manifests.\n- `experiments/` contains executable or fully specified benchmarks and tests.\n- `deliverables/` contains the 18 requested outputs and the final report.\n\n## Operations\n\n### Ingest\n\n1. Read `wiki/index.md` and search the wiki for the source's concepts.\n2. Add a stable source identifier (`SRC-####`) to the inventory.\n3. Acquire the source into `raw/` and record provenance and SHA-256.\n4. Classify authority and lifecycle scope before extracting claims.\n5. Update the smallest applicable existing wiki pages.\n6. Link claims to their source receipt and, where relevant, pinned code.\n7. Record disagreements in `wiki/contradictions.md`.\n8. Update `wiki/index.md` and append an entry to `wiki/log.md`.\n\n### Query\n\nRead `wiki/index.md`, then the smallest relevant linked pages and receipts.\nReturn cited synthesis. If the answer establishes reusable knowledge, merge it\ninto the wiki and append a query entry to the log.\n\n### Lint\n\nCheck required metadata, unique page IDs, broken relative links, source IDs,\norphan pages, duplicate concepts, stale moving facts, missing commit hashes,\nunresolved contradictions, unsupported status levels, and mismatches between\nthe index and on-disk pages.\n\n## Page metadata\n\nEvery maintained page except `index.md` and `log.md` starts with:\n\n```yaml\n---\nid: stable.dotted.identifier\ntype: overview|source|component|semantics|formal|runtime|language|security|benchmark|comparison|adoption|governance|decision|question|contradiction\ntitle: Plain title\nstatus: active|blocked|superseded|resolved\nupdated_at: YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ\nsources:\n  - SRC-####\n---\n```\n\n## Claim record\n\nEvery material claim must make the following recoverable from its paragraph,\ntable row, or adjacent claim block:\n\n- stable claim ID (`CLM-####`);\n- exact source ID and locator;\n- publication, release, deployment, or commit date;\n- repository, tag, branch, and full commit hash where relevant;\n- authority: normative, descriptive, historical, experimental, or promotional;\n- scope: V1, deployed validator, current service, unreleased implementation,\n  V2.0 proposal, V2.1 proposal, or speculative future;\n- evidence kind: source fact, repository observation, experiment observation,\n  inference, recommendation, contradiction, or open question;\n- reproduction: reproduced, partially reproduced, not reproduced, or not\n  applicable;\n- confidence: high, medium, low, or unknown;\n- lifecycle status S0 through S7.\n\nUse these lifecycle meanings exactly:\n\n- `S0`: historical or superseded.\n- `S1`: idea or discussion only.\n- `S2`: written proposal or design.\n- `S3`: prototype or experimental branch.\n- `S4`: implemented and tested but unreleased.\n- `S5`: released but not deployed or broadly supported.\n- `S6`: deployed and supported.\n- `S7`: deployed, independently audited, and operationally evidenced.\n\nStatus is scoped to the claim, not assigned wholesale to a repository.\n\n## Source receipt fields\n\nEach acquired web source requires requested URL, canonical URL, retrieval UTC,\nHTTP status, publication or last-modified date if known, source class, local\npath, SHA-256, acquisition method, relevant version, and coverage limitations.\n\nRepository receipts additionally require remote, default branch, checked-out\nbranch or tag, full commit, submodule state, dirty state, and acquisition UTC.\n\n## Contradictions and moving facts\n\nDo not select the newest-looking source silently. Record each conflicting claim,\nthe scopes that may explain the conflict, reproduction evidence, and the\nprovisional disposition. Dynamic facts such as protocol parameters, deployment\nstatus, open issue counts, or branch heads must carry an `observed_at` time and\nmust not be copied into multiple canonical locations.\n\n## Formal and empirical language\n\nState guarantees in theorem-like form with assumptions. Keep semantic\nnon-locking distinct from validator acceptance, ledger-resource feasibility,\ntransaction-construction feasibility, continuation availability, participant\ncooperation and keys, wallet/Runtime correctness, and future protocol support.\n\nUse `specified-only` for an experiment design that has not run. Use `reproduced`\nonly when commands, inputs, environment, raw outputs, and acceptance predicates\nare preserved.\n<deep_research_assignment>\n\n  <title>\n    Modernizing Marlowe:\n    A Decision-Grade Language, Formal-Semantics, Runtime, Tooling,\n    Governance, and Adoption Strategy for Marlowe V2 and Beyond\n  </title>\n\n  <research_date>2026-09-02</research_date>\n\n  <role>\n    Act as a multidisciplinary principal research and architecture team\n    combining the expertise of:\n\n    - a programming-language designer specializing in restricted,\n      domain-specific, and total languages;\n    - a formal-methods researcher experienced with Isabelle, Agda, Lean,\n      mechanized operational semantics, compiler correctness, and model checking;\n    - a Cardano ledger, extended-UTxO, Plutus, and transaction-construction\n      architect;\n    - a compiler, type-system, intermediate-representation, and language-server\n      engineer;\n    - a distributed-systems and application-runtime architect;\n    - a smart-contract security researcher and independent auditor;\n    - a financial-contract-domain expert familiar with ACTUS, derivatives,\n      escrow, lending, settlement, and institutional workflows;\n    - a developer-tools, low-code, visual-programming, and API designer;\n    - an open-source maintainer, release engineer, and technical-governance\n      specialist;\n    - a product strategist studying developer adoption and financial-platform\n      product-market fit;\n    - and a technical facilitator capable of turning research findings into a\n      structured stakeholder discussion and an actionable decision record.\n  </role>\n\n  <mission>\n    Produce a decision-grade modernization study and stakeholder discussion\n    packet for Marlowe.\n\n    Determine the strongest evolution of Marlowe that:\n\n    1. preserves or strengthens its high-assurance financial-contract thesis;\n    2. materially improves expressiveness, readability, modularity,\n       scalability, composability, and developer experience;\n    3. remains executable and economically practical under current and\n       realistically anticipated Cardano ledger constraints;\n    4. cleanly separates the stable language semantics from Cardano-specific\n       validator and Runtime concerns;\n    5. has an explicit formal-verification and security-assurance strategy;\n    6. provides a credible migration path from existing Marlowe V1 contracts,\n       validators, APIs, serialized artifacts, and applications;\n    7. is maintainable by the present community and governance structure;\n    8. responds to demonstrated user and application needs rather than producing\n       an abstract language-design wishlist;\n    9. incorporates, audits, and where necessary revises the existing Marlowe V2\n       report and implementation proposals;\n    10. identifies the exact scope that should ship as V2.0, what should be\n        developed for V2.1, what belongs in a later version or extension layer,\n        and what should be rejected.\n  </mission>\n\n  <central_question>\n    What should Marlowe become over the next three years if its purpose is to be\n    the safest, most analyzable, usable, and operationally credible language and\n    platform for finite financial agreements on Cardano and potentially other\n    execution environments?\n\n    Do not answer this by simply adding features to Marlowe V1. Decide which\n    capabilities belong in:\n\n    - the minimal semantic kernel;\n    - a typed source language that elaborates into the kernel;\n    - reusable libraries and contract templates;\n    - the Cardano validator layer;\n    - transaction-composition protocols;\n    - Marlowe Runtime;\n    - external oracle, identity, and token-policy services;\n    - or application-specific code.\n  </central_question>\n\n  <starting_position>\n    Treat Marlowe as a complete socio-technical platform rather than merely a\n    syntax.\n\n    The research must reconstruct and independently verify the following\n    baseline:\n\n    - the current Marlowe V1 contract and action algebra;\n    - its executable semantics and transaction-reduction model;\n    - accounts, parties, addresses, roles, role tokens, payouts, values,\n      observations, timeouts, warnings, and errors;\n    - contract continuations, object bundles, shallow and deep Merkleization,\n      and continuation storage;\n    - the relationship between abstract semantics, the Haskell implementation,\n      Plutus validators, Cardano transactions, and ledger rules;\n    - the Runtime service architecture, APIs, transaction construction,\n      indexing, chain synchronization, and rollback handling;\n    - the TypeScript SDK, Playground, Blockly support, Runner, CLI,\n      MarloweScan, starter kit, examples, and ACTUS tooling;\n    - the Isabelle and Agda specifications and the extent to which they are\n      authoritative, executable, synchronized, and complete;\n    - audits, static analysis, property-based testing, golden tests,\n      differential tests, and on-chain tests;\n    - the Marlowe Language CIC, SIG/community processes, MIPs, release\n      practices, repository ownership, and maintenance capacity;\n    - and the existing Marlowe V2 design report and funding proposals.\n\n    Do not assume that documentation, code, formal specifications, deployed\n    validators, package releases, and current services all describe the same\n    version. Reconcile them explicitly.\n  </starting_position>\n\n  <candidate_enduring_thesis>\n    Evaluate, rather than blindly assume, that a modern Marlowe should retain\n    these principles:\n\n    1. Marlowe is a financial-agreement DSL, not a general-purpose smart-contract\n       language.\n    2. Every contract is finite or otherwise has a statically provable resource\n       and lifetime bound.\n    3. All branches, waits, and non-cooperation contingencies are explicit.\n    4. Contract behavior is inspectable by domain experts and analyzable by\n       machines.\n    5. Value conservation and payout behavior are part of the semantic model.\n    6. The maximum lifetime and execution-resource envelope can be determined\n       before deployment.\n    7. A universal or small family of audited validators can execute many\n       contracts without each contract requiring a new audit.\n    8. High-level source convenience must not enlarge the trusted on-chain kernel\n       without strong justification.\n    9. Off-chain services may improve usability, but correctness should not\n       silently depend on trusting a particular Runtime operator.\n    10. The distinction among semantic safety, ledger executability, participant\n        liveness, data availability, and application security must remain\n        explicit.\n  </candidate_enduring_thesis>\n\n  <mandatory_source_corpus>\n\n    <official_entry_points>\n      Inspect all sections, links, guides, tutorials, playbooks, examples,\n      developer tools, and security material reachable from:\n\n      https://docs.cardano.org/developer-resources/smart-contracts/marlowe\n      https://docs.marlowe-lang.org/\n      https://marlowe-lang.org/\n      https://marlowe-lang.org/blog/\n    </official_entry_points>\n\n    <marlowe_v2_sources>\n      Inspect in full:\n\n      https://marlowe-lang.org/blog/marlowe-v2-sessions-report/\n      https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Ex7SyN2hys8CD-hmULiwYNkKumGELlEAl15KWkMIpKs/edit\n      https://projectcatalyst.io/funds/13/cardano-use-cases-concept/marlowe-2025-marlowe-v2\n      https://hydra-voting.intersectmbo.org/votes/cardano-budget-2026/69fc8a71b05ff80adc7d5c4f\n\n      Locate meeting notes, recordings, follow-up posts, prototypes, branches,\n      pull requests, work packages, milestone reports, funding decisions, and\n      implementation status associated with these sources.\n\n      Do not treat a proposal, workshop conclusion, requested budget, merged\n      prototype, deployed validator, or production-supported release as\n      equivalent statuses.\n    </marlowe_v2_sources>\n\n    <repositories>\n      Inspect the complete Marlowe GitHub organization:\n\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang\n\n      At minimum, deeply inspect:\n\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-agda\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-cardano\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-plutus\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-runtime-ng\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-ts-sdk\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-playground\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-runner\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-doc\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-starter-kit\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/MIPs\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/actus-core\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/marlowe-oracle-protocol\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/real-world-marlowe\n      https://github.com/marlowe-lang/awesome-marlowe\n\n      Enumerate every repository in the organization and classify it as:\n\n      - active and authoritative;\n      - active but experimental;\n      - maintenance-only;\n      - superseded;\n      - archived;\n      - demonstration/example material;\n      - infrastructure or deployment support;\n      - formal specification;\n      - client library;\n      - or unclear.\n\n      Record latest meaningful commit dates, releases, tags, open issues,\n      pull requests, maintainers, language, dependency relationships, and\n      apparent replacement repositories.\n\n      Inspect source code rather than relying only on repository READMEs.\n    </repositories>\n\n    <formal_and_assurance_sources>\n      Inspect:\n\n      - the Isabelle Marlowe specification and proof scripts;\n      - the Agda implementation and any V2 prototypes;\n      - the on-chain transaction specification;\n      - the validator test report;\n      - Marlowe security guidance;\n      - audit reports and remediation records;\n      - static-analysis implementation;\n      - property, golden, semantics-oracle, fuzz, integration, and on-chain\n        tests;\n      - validator script registries and hash-verification mechanisms;\n      - and all research papers linked by the official Marlowe documentation.\n\n      Include at minimum the papers concerning:\n\n      - Marlowe financial contracts on blockchain;\n      - implementation and analysis of Marlowe;\n      - efficient static analysis;\n      - standardized crypto loans;\n      - and scripting smart contracts for distributed ledgers.\n    </formal_and_assurance_sources>\n\n    <cardano_platform_sources>\n      Use current primary Cardano sources to establish:\n\n      - the current ledger era and active Plutus versions;\n      - current protocol parameters and transaction limits;\n      - inline datums, reference inputs, reference scripts, collateral, native\n        assets, minting policies, transaction metadata, wallet interfaces, and\n        governance-era capabilities;\n      - current and scheduled hard-fork changes that affect scripts, transaction\n        composition, nested transactions, execution limits, or state-machine\n        design;\n      - and the current state of Hydra and other relevant Cardano L2 mechanisms.\n\n      Never copy historical numerical limits from an old Marlowe page without\n      checking the live protocol parameters and their effective dates.\n    </cardano_platform_sources>\n\n    <empirical_sources>\n      Inspect:\n\n      - MarloweScan and raw on-chain data;\n      - deployed validator versions and script hashes;\n      - mainnet, pre-production, and preview contract activity;\n      - contract templates and application repositories;\n      - Runtime API use;\n      - package-download and repository-activity signals;\n      - issue trackers, support questions, Discord/SIG discussions when\n        accessible, and developer interviews;\n      - and known organizations or applications that have evaluated or used\n        Marlowe.\n\n      Treat absence of public evidence as absence of evidence, not proof of\n      absence. Clearly identify unverifiable adoption claims.\n    </empirical_sources>\n\n    <comparative_sources>\n      Compare Marlowe with current primary documentation and research for:\n\n      - Aiken, PlutusTx, Plinth, Scalus, and other relevant Cardano languages;\n      - DAML and Canton;\n      - ACTUS and other financial-contract standards;\n      - Findel-style financial contract combinators;\n      - Pact;\n      - Move;\n      - Scrypto;\n      - Scilla;\n      - Solidity and Vyper;\n      - Michelson, LIGO, SmartPy, and Archetype;\n      - Reach;\n      - Simplicity;\n      - BitML and state-channel contract languages;\n      - session types, typestate, effect systems, linear types, refinement\n        types, and temporal-contract languages;\n      - workflow and business-process languages;\n      - and verified compiler or proof-carrying-code architectures.\n\n      Do not produce a superficial feature checklist. Determine which design\n      ideas transfer to Marlowe without destroying its bounded, analyzable\n      character.\n    </comparative_sources>\n\n  </mandatory_source_corpus>\n\n  <evidence_and_status_rules>\n    For every material claim, record:\n\n    - exact source;\n    - publication or commit date;\n    - repository, tag, branch, and commit hash where relevant;\n    - whether the source is normative, descriptive, historical, experimental,\n      or promotional;\n    - whether the claim describes V1, a deployed validator, a current service,\n      an unreleased implementation, the V2.0 proposal, a V2.1 idea, or a\n      speculative future direction;\n    - whether the claim was independently reproduced;\n    - and confidence level.\n\n    Use this status taxonomy consistently:\n\n    S0 = historical or superseded;\n    S1 = idea or discussion only;\n    S2 = written proposal or design;\n    S3 = prototype or experimental branch;\n    S4 = implemented and tested but unreleased;\n    S5 = released but not deployed or broadly supported;\n    S6 = deployed and supported;\n    S7 = deployed, independently audited, and operationally evidenced.\n\n    Resolve contradictions among documentation, code, formal specifications,\n    APIs, release notes, and deployed scripts. Do not silently choose the\n    newest-looking source.\n  </evidence_and_status_rules>\n\n  <workstream_1_current_system_reconstruction>\n    Reconstruct Marlowe V1 precisely.\n\n    Produce:\n\n    1. The complete abstract syntax and data model for contracts, actions,\n       inputs, values, observations, parties, payees, tokens, accounts, choices,\n       timeouts, state, warnings, payments, and transaction results.\n    2. The operational flow through interval fixing, reduction to quiescence,\n       input application, transaction computation, and continuation selection.\n    3. The meaning and consequences of each warning and error.\n    4. The relationship among source representations, JSON encoding, object\n       bundles, linked objects, contract hashes, and Merkleized continuations.\n    5. The lifecycle of a contract from authoring through simulation, analysis,\n       deployment, transaction construction, validation, indexing, payout, and\n       closure.\n    6. The exact authorization model for addresses, roles, role tokens, open\n       roles, and payouts.\n    7. The assumptions that make the abstract semantics blockchain-agnostic and\n       the points at which the Cardano realization is not blockchain-agnostic.\n    8. The trusted computing base for each supported user workflow.\n    9. A system-context diagram, component diagram, transaction sequence\n       diagram, and data/provenance diagram.\n  </workstream_1_current_system_reconstruction>\n\n  <workstream_2_formal_guarantee_audit>\n    Build a formal-guarantee matrix with columns:\n\n    - claimed property;\n    - exact mathematical statement;\n    - environmental assumptions;\n    - specification containing the property;\n    - proof assistant or test mechanism;\n    - proof status;\n    - implementation correspondence;\n    - Cardano realization correspondence;\n    - known counterexample or qualification;\n    - V2 impact;\n    - and required new proof work.\n\n    Audit at least:\n\n    - termination;\n    - finite maximum lifetime;\n    - conservation of value;\n    - positivity of internal accounts;\n    - eventual closure;\n    - absence of value after closure;\n    - quiescence;\n    - idempotence of reduction to quiescence;\n    - equivalence of grouped and separately applied inputs;\n    - determinism;\n    - upper bounds on transactions and execution;\n    - authorization soundness;\n    - timeout liveness;\n    - payout accessibility;\n    - serialization and hashing consistency;\n    - and correspondence among Isabelle, Agda, Haskell, TypeScript, and Plutus.\n\n    Replace unconditional marketing formulations with theorem-like statements\n    that expose assumptions.\n\n    In particular, distinguish:\n\n    A. semantic non-locking;\n    B. validator acceptance;\n    C. ledger-resource feasibility;\n    D. transaction-construction feasibility;\n    E. availability of Merkleized continuations;\n    F. participant willingness and key availability;\n    G. wallet and Runtime correctness;\n    H. and continued availability of an execution path under protocol changes.\n\n    Determine whether one formalization should become normative and whether\n    other implementations should be generated from it, proved equivalent to it,\n    or checked through a conformance suite.\n  </workstream_2_formal_guarantee_audit>\n\n  <workstream_3_repository_and_implementation_archaeology>\n    Build a repository and package dependency graph.\n\n    Identify:\n\n    - duplicated implementations of Marlowe types or semantics;\n    - generated versus hand-maintained code;\n    - version-skew risks;\n    - abandoned integrations;\n    - build-system and Nix complexity;\n    - validator compiler dependencies;\n    - serialization boundaries;\n    - API compatibility constraints;\n    - unmaintained client libraries;\n    - gaps in release automation;\n    - absence or presence of security policies;\n    - test ownership;\n    - and single-maintainer or funding risks.\n\n    Determine which repositories should be:\n\n    - retained as authoritative;\n    - merged;\n    - split;\n    - generated from specifications;\n    - rewritten;\n    - archived;\n    - or replaced.\n\n    Propose a coherent target package and repository architecture for V2.\n  </workstream_3_repository_and_implementation_archaeology>\n\n  <workstream_4_empirical_contract_and_cost_study>\n    Implement or reconstruct a representative benchmark suite containing:\n\n    - simple escrow;\n    - atomic token swap;\n    - zero-coupon bond;\n    - recurring loan or interest schedule;\n    - covered call or option;\n    - auction;\n    - contract for differences using oracle input;\n    - multi-party revenue distribution;\n    - milestone-based grant or treasury contract;\n    - dynamically joined participant or open-role contract;\n    - long-running subscription;\n    - ACTUS-derived contract;\n    - and a high-fan-out close or payout scenario.\n\n    For V1 and each serious modernization candidate, measure:\n\n    - source size and readability;\n    - expanded AST size;\n    - serialized JSON and datum size;\n    - object-bundle and Merkleized size;\n    - number of continuations;\n    - number of transactions;\n    - script execution units;\n    - transaction size;\n    - memory use;\n    - fees;\n    - minimum-UTxO implications;\n    - number of internal accounts and payouts;\n    - Runtime calls;\n    - wallet signatures;\n    - continuation-storage requirements;\n    - failure behavior;\n    - analysis time;\n    - and ability to calculate safe upper bounds before deployment.\n\n    Test several wallet UTxO configurations rather than reporting idealized\n    transactions only.\n\n    Reproduce resource-limit failures and determine whether each proposed\n    mitigation moves, removes, or merely obscures the limit.\n  </workstream_4_empirical_contract_and_cost_study>\n\n  <workstream_5_language_usability_and_user_research>\n    Evaluate Marlowe separately for:\n\n    - financial-domain experts with limited programming experience;\n    - smart-contract developers;\n    - TypeScript application developers;\n    - auditors and formal-methods specialists;\n    - infrastructure operators;\n    - and institutional legal, risk, and operations teams.\n\n    Investigate:\n\n    - whether the existing concrete syntax is actually used;\n    - whether most contracts are instead constructed in Haskell, TypeScript,\n      JavaScript, Blockly, JSON, or templates;\n    - where authoring errors occur;\n    - where users lose the connection between source intent and on-chain state;\n    - whether Blockly and textual representations round-trip safely;\n    - whether users understand accounts, roles, payouts, timeouts, and\n      Merkleization;\n    - how easily users interpret static-analysis counterexamples;\n    - how much Cardano and Plutus knowledge remains necessary;\n    - and whether Runtime abstractions reduce complexity or merely relocate it.\n\n    Conduct structured interviews or derive an interview protocol. Do not infer\n    usability solely from language elegance.\n\n    Identify the smallest set of use cases for which Marlowe is clearly better\n    than writing an Aiken or Plutus application directly.\n  </workstream_5_language_usability_and_user_research>\n\n  <workstream_6_existing_v2_proposal_audit>\n    Reconstruct every proposal in the final Marlowe V2 report and related\n    implementation proposals.\n\n    At minimum, investigate:\n\n    - changing timeout behavior so that When closes by default or always closes;\n    - waiting for all, any, threshold, or otherwise structured sets of actions;\n    - WhenAll or equivalent constructs;\n    - EntryDeposit and replacement of the separate open-role validator;\n    - state compression;\n    - improved Close behavior for large participant sets;\n    - bounded iteration;\n    - cryptographic and hashing primitives;\n    - finer-grained types or kinds for values;\n    - token-aware arithmetic;\n    - time and duration arithmetic;\n    - token-minting sublanguages;\n    - oracle improvements;\n    - Runtime and tooling changes;\n    - validator restructuring;\n    - and Marlowe execution in L2 or state-channel environments.\n\n    For each proposal provide:\n\n    - problem being solved;\n    - evidence that the problem is material;\n    - current workaround;\n    - exact proposed semantics;\n    - alternative designs;\n    - dependencies;\n    - source-compatibility impact;\n    - serialized-data impact;\n    - validator and Runtime impact;\n    - formal proof obligations;\n    - execution-cost impact;\n    - security implications;\n    - migration implications;\n    - implementation status;\n    - and recommendation:\n      retain, revise, split, merge, defer, reject, or move outside the core.\n\n    Do not preserve the V2.0, V2.1, and “possible” classifications merely\n    because they appeared in the workshop report. Reassess them with evidence.\n  </workstream_6_existing_v2_proposal_audit>\n\n  <workstream_7_modernization_architectures>\n    Develop and compare at least these four coherent alternatives.\n\n    <option_0_v1_stabilization>\n      Maintain the existing language semantics, concentrating on documentation,\n      compatibility, Runtime simplification, cost improvements, security,\n      tooling, and governance.\n\n      Treat this as a serious baseline rather than a straw man.\n    </option_0_v1_stabilization>\n\n    <option_1_conservative_v2>\n      Evolve the existing core directly with a tightly limited set of V2\n      constructs, broadly following the official V2.0 proposal.\n\n      Optimize for continuity, reduced implementation burden, and preservation\n      of existing mental and formal models.\n    </option_1_conservative_v2>\n\n    <option_2_verified_core_and_typed_surface>\n      Define a smaller or comparably small Marlowe Core 2 as the authoritative\n      executable kernel.\n\n      Add a modern typed surface language that may provide:\n\n      - modules and imports;\n      - named definitions;\n      - parameterized templates;\n      - typed functions or macros that elaborate away;\n      - records and domain data used at compile time;\n      - generics over parties, tokens, and schedules;\n      - bounded loops, folds, and comprehensions;\n      - relative-time expressions;\n      - typed amounts and units;\n      - pattern-like construction;\n      - reusable packages;\n      - and better diagnostics.\n\n      Require elaboration to produce a finite core contract with an explicit\n      resource, transaction, and lifetime bound before deployment.\n\n      Investigate whether the surface compiler can be verified, proof-producing,\n      translation-validated, or sufficiently checked through differential and\n      property testing.\n    </option_2_verified_core_and_typed_surface>\n\n    <option_3_composable_protocol_dsl>\n      Treat Marlowe as a typed financial state-machine and transaction-intent\n      language that can safely compose with external validators, minting\n      policies, credentials, oracles, and application logic.\n\n      Define explicit capability and trust boundaries rather than embedding\n      arbitrary Plutus behavior inside the Marlowe semantic kernel.\n\n      Investigate typed foreign calls, transaction constraints, effect\n      declarations, capability manifests, script-hash allowlists, and\n      compositional audit evidence.\n    </option_3_composable_protocol_dsl>\n\n    <option_4_portable_or_l2_semantics>\n      Explore whether the blockchain-agnostic semantics should have multiple\n      execution backends, including Cardano L1, Hydra or another state-channel\n      environment, or additional ledgers.\n\n      Do not recommend portability merely because it is technically possible.\n      Identify the common semantic substrate, unavoidable backend differences,\n      security models, liquidity assumptions, and realistic demand.\n    </option_4_portable_or_l2_semantics>\n\n    Score all options using the decision framework below. Hybrid proposals are\n    permitted, but their boundaries must be exact.\n  </workstream_7_modernization_architectures>\n\n  <workstream_8_language_design_questions>\n\n    <types>\n      Determine whether V2 needs:\n\n      - distinct types for amounts, integers, ratios, percentages, timestamps,\n        durations, slot or POSIX time, token quantities, account balances,\n        choice values, hashes, signatures, credentials, and oracle observations;\n      - token-indexed or unit-indexed amounts;\n      - refinement types for ranges and non-negativity;\n      - linear or affine treatment of value and authorization;\n      - effect types for deposits, payments, minting, oracle reads, and external\n        script interaction;\n      - typestate for contract phases;\n      - and explicit uncertainty or freshness metadata for oracle values.\n\n      Show which checks occur at authoring, elaboration, static analysis,\n      Runtime transaction construction, and on chain.\n    </types>\n\n    <control_and_concurrency>\n      Specify the semantics of:\n\n      - default closure on timeout;\n      - explicit non-Close timeout continuations, if retained;\n      - all-of, any-of, ordered, unordered, threshold, and atomic action sets;\n      - simultaneous deposits;\n      - competing choices;\n      - cancellation;\n      - bounded iteration;\n      - bounded retries;\n      - and multi-party progress.\n\n      Explain ordering, race conditions, front-running implications, quiescence,\n      fairness, and transaction atomicity.\n    </control_and_concurrency>\n\n    <abstraction_and_packages>\n      Evaluate:\n\n      - modules;\n      - imports;\n      - package identifiers and versions;\n      - named reusable components;\n      - parameterized contracts;\n      - hygienic macros;\n      - compile-time functions;\n      - generics;\n      - schedule and payment combinators;\n      - interface or trait-like abstractions;\n      - object bundles;\n      - canonical linking;\n      - dependency locking;\n      - reproducible builds;\n      - and package signing.\n\n      Prevent abstraction features from introducing hidden unbounded execution.\n    </abstraction_and_packages>\n\n    <time>\n      Design a coherent distinction among:\n\n      - absolute ledger time;\n      - relative durations;\n      - business calendars;\n      - recurring schedules;\n      - settlement windows;\n      - grace periods;\n      - and oracle publication times.\n\n      Identify what belongs in consensus semantics versus off-chain schedule\n      generation. Address daylight-saving time, calendar conventions, and\n      external business-day rules without pretending the ledger natively knows\n      them.\n    </time>\n\n    <parties_identity_and_authorization>\n      Compare:\n\n      - fixed addresses;\n      - transferable role tokens;\n      - open roles;\n      - EntryDeposit;\n      - multisignature or threshold authorization;\n      - native scripts;\n      - script-controlled credentials;\n      - identity credentials;\n      - delegated authorization;\n      - recovery and key rotation;\n      - and revocation.\n\n      Determine whether roles should remain token-based capabilities or become a\n      more general typed authorization abstraction.\n\n      Preserve visibility of the exact monetary-policy and script assumptions.\n    </parties_identity_and_authorization>\n\n    <oracles_and_external_data>\n      Define how contracts represent:\n\n      - data identifiers;\n      - source identity;\n      - signatures or attestations;\n      - timestamp and freshness;\n      - aggregation;\n      - dispute or fallback behavior;\n      - replay protection;\n      - bounds and units;\n      - and oracle failure.\n\n      Compare direct Choice-based inputs with the emerging Marlowe oracle\n      protocol and Cardano-wide oracle patterns.\n    </oracles_and_external_data>\n\n    <minting_and_external_scripts>\n      Decide whether token minting and interaction with non-Marlowe validators\n      belong in:\n\n      - core semantics;\n      - a restricted sublanguage;\n      - a typed transaction-composition layer;\n      - capability-declared extensions;\n      - or application code.\n\n      Analyze the audit and proof boundary of each approach.\n\n      Do not imply that arbitrary external scripts inherit Marlowe's safety\n      guarantees.\n    </minting_and_external_scripts>\n\n    <errors_warnings_and_obligations>\n      Replace loosely structured warnings where appropriate with typed,\n      attributable diagnostics.\n\n      Investigate whether Marlowe should express and analyze:\n\n      - hard errors;\n      - warnings;\n      - unmet obligations;\n      - partial payments;\n      - infeasible branches;\n      - unreachable actions;\n      - liveness dependencies;\n      - insufficient collateral or minimum value;\n      - resource-bound violations;\n      - and reliance on external data or scripts.\n    </errors_warnings_and_obligations>\n\n    <serialization_and_versioning>\n      Specify:\n\n      - canonical source and core encodings;\n      - stable hashes;\n      - schema identifiers;\n      - language and validator version identifiers;\n      - forward and backward compatibility;\n      - unknown-field behavior;\n      - package and continuation hashes;\n      - source maps;\n      - and cross-language conformance.\n\n      Analyze whether existing JSON is suitable as a long-term canonical\n      interchange format.\n    </serialization_and_versioning>\n\n  </workstream_8_language_design_questions>\n\n  <workstream_9_scalability_and_state>\n    Analyze separately:\n\n    - semantic contract size;\n    - serialized contract size;\n    - on-chain datum size;\n    - transaction size;\n    - validator execution memory and CPU;\n    - number of internal accounts;\n    - payout fan-out;\n    - wallet-input overhead;\n    - continuation lookup;\n    - Runtime database state;\n    - and chain-index requirements.\n\n    Evaluate:\n\n    - state compression;\n    - continuation DAG normalization;\n    - improved Merkleization;\n    - canonical subtree sharing;\n    - proof-carrying continuation retrieval;\n    - replicated or decentralized continuation storage;\n    - staged payouts;\n    - pull-based payout queues;\n    - aggregation;\n    - batched close;\n    - account compaction;\n    - reference inputs and scripts;\n    - nested transactions where applicable;\n    - and L2 execution.\n\n    For each optimization state:\n\n    - what resource is reduced;\n    - what new resource or trust dependency is introduced;\n    - whether auditability is preserved;\n    - whether data availability becomes weaker;\n    - and whether the optimization changes semantics or only realization.\n  </workstream_9_scalability_and_state>\n\n  <workstream_10_runtime_and_api_modernization>\n    Compare the existing Runtime architecture with the lightweight\n    marlowe-runtime-ng direction and alternative architectures.\n\n    Determine whether V2 Runtime should be:\n\n    - a monolithic service;\n    - a set of independently deployable services;\n    - a stateless transaction planner plus replaceable chain/index providers;\n    - a client-side or edge-capable library;\n    - or a hybrid.\n\n    Cover:\n\n    - transaction planning;\n    - coin and UTxO selection;\n    - fee and execution-unit estimation;\n    - safety checking before contract creation;\n    - continuation storage;\n    - contract discovery and indexing;\n    - rollback behavior;\n    - payout discovery;\n    - event subscriptions;\n    - wallet signing;\n    - partial transaction workflows;\n    - hardware and enterprise custody;\n    - script registries;\n    - REST and WebSocket API versioning;\n    - OpenAPI generation;\n    - typed clients;\n    - idempotency;\n    - retries;\n    - observability;\n    - provenance;\n    - multi-provider operation;\n    - local verification of Runtime output;\n    - and trust-minimized deployment.\n\n    Establish what a client must verify so that a compromised or faulty Runtime\n    cannot induce an unintended contract or transaction.\n  </workstream_10_runtime_and_api_modernization>\n\n  <workstream_11_tooling_and_authoring>\n    Define a complete modern developer environment, including:\n\n    - a canonical textual syntax;\n    - parser, formatter, and linter;\n    - language server;\n    - semantic highlighting;\n    - type-directed completion;\n    - source maps from surface language to core and on-chain data;\n    - visual execution traces;\n    - time-travel simulation;\n    - debugger;\n    - symbolic path exploration;\n    - static-analysis counterexamples;\n    - property testing;\n    - contract-difference and semantic-equivalence tools;\n    - cost and transaction-bound visualization;\n    - visual/textual round-trip editing;\n    - package manager and registry;\n    - reproducible builds;\n    - CI integration;\n    - deployment manifests;\n    - script-hash verification;\n    - and contract provenance or software-bill-of-materials output.\n\n    Evaluate AI-assisted contract authoring, but place the LLM outside the\n    trusted core.\n\n    An AI-generated contract must pass the same parser, type checker,\n    elaborator, analyzer, simulator, conformance tests, and user approval as\n    human-written source. Propose methods for producing explanations,\n    assumptions, counterexamples, and proof evidence rather than trusting an\n    LLM's natural-language assurances.\n  </workstream_11_tooling_and_authoring>\n\n  <workstream_12_security_and_threat_model>\n    Produce an end-to-end threat model covering:\n\n    - malicious or incorrectly configured role-token monetary policies;\n    - counterfeit or unsupported validator versions;\n    - script-registry compromise;\n    - invalid initial state;\n    - malformed addresses or tokens;\n    - arithmetic and unit confusion;\n    - timeout ambiguity;\n    - unavailable Merkleized continuations;\n    - continuation substitution;\n    - oracle manipulation, staleness, equivocation, and replay;\n    - external-script composition;\n    - Runtime compromise;\n    - transaction substitution;\n    - man-in-the-middle and web-application attacks;\n    - wallet compromise;\n    - cross-site scripting;\n    - denial of service and resource exhaustion;\n    - chain rollback;\n    - API version skew;\n    - serialization disagreement;\n    - package and dependency compromise;\n    - compiler or validator miscompilation;\n    - governance capture;\n    - and upgrade confusion.\n\n    For each threat identify:\n\n    - asset;\n    - adversary;\n    - trust boundary;\n    - precondition;\n    - attack path;\n    - consequence;\n    - detection;\n    - prevention;\n    - recovery;\n    - and residual risk.\n\n    Specify independent-audit boundaries for the semantic kernel, compiler,\n    validators, Runtime, SDK, and extension mechanisms.\n  </workstream_12_security_and_threat_model>\n\n  <workstream_13_conformance_and_verification_strategy>\n    Design a sustainable assurance pipeline containing:\n\n    - a normative language specification;\n    - executable reference semantics;\n    - formal proofs;\n    - validator correspondence;\n    - cross-language conformance vectors;\n    - differential testing;\n    - property-based generation of correlated state, contract, and input data;\n    - fuzzing of parsers, serializers, linkers, and APIs;\n    - mutation testing;\n    - golden examples;\n    - on-chain tests;\n    - cost-regression tests;\n    - compatibility tests;\n    - and reproducible validator builds.\n\n    Consider these implementation strategies:\n\n    1. Isabelle remains normative and other implementations are checked against\n       it.\n    2. Agda becomes the executable and formal reference.\n    3. A new specification is written in Lean or another environment.\n    4. A compact reference interpreter is normative while proof artifacts\n       establish its properties.\n    5. Multiple independent semantics implementations are retained and checked\n       through differential conformance.\n\n    Recommend one strategy based on proof reuse, extraction quality,\n    maintainability, contributor availability, integration with the compiler,\n    and risk of specification drift.\n\n    Define the V2 proof obligations before implementation begins.\n  </workstream_13_conformance_and_verification_strategy>\n\n  <workstream_14_migration_and_compatibility>\n    Assume that existing on-chain V1 contracts and validators are immutable.\n\n    Design:\n\n    - side-by-side V1 and V2 operation;\n    - version negotiation;\n    - validator and payout-script registries;\n    - Runtime routing;\n    - API compatibility;\n    - SDK compatibility;\n    - serialized-contract identification;\n    - continuation-store compatibility;\n    - explorer support;\n    - and end-of-support policy.\n\n    Classify V1 contracts into:\n\n    - mechanically translatable with proven semantic equivalence;\n    - translatable only with user-selected policy decisions;\n    - representable but behaviorally changed;\n    - or not safely translatable.\n\n    Provide:\n\n    - a V1-to-V2 translator design;\n    - an equivalence checker;\n    - source and state migration reports;\n    - dry-run tooling;\n    - rollback procedures;\n    - and guidance for contracts that should remain on V1.\n\n    Do not use “breaking change” as a substitute for a migration strategy.\n  </workstream_14_migration_and_compatibility>\n\n  <workstream_15_adoption_and_product_strategy>\n    Determine who Marlowe V2 is for.\n\n    Test candidate target segments such as:\n\n    - Cardano DApp developers who want audited financial state machines;\n    - treasury and grant-management systems;\n    - escrow and marketplace applications;\n    - token vesting and distribution;\n    - loans, bonds, derivatives, and structured products;\n    - institutional settlement workflows;\n    - fintech developers using TypeScript;\n    - legal or financial professionals using templates or visual tools;\n    - educational users;\n    - and multi-chain financial-contract platforms.\n\n    For each segment assess:\n\n    - current workflow;\n    - unmet need;\n    - alternative solution;\n    - reason to choose Marlowe;\n    - integration burden;\n    - assurance requirement;\n    - regulatory or operational concern;\n    - willingness to adopt;\n    - and measurable success criteria.\n\n    Investigate whether “non-programmers author smart contracts” remains the\n    principal value proposition or whether the stronger proposition is\n    “developers compose audited, analyzable financial state machines.”\n\n    Recommend a precise positioning statement and three to five flagship\n    applications that exercise the modernization rather than merely demonstrate\n    toy examples.\n  </workstream_15_adoption_and_product_strategy>\n\n  <decision_scorecard>\n    Score each modernization architecture from 0 to 5 using this weighted\n    framework:\n\n    - semantic safety and formal assurance: 20%;\n    - ledger executability and predictable cost: 15%;\n    - useful financial expressiveness: 12%;\n    - authoring and debugging experience: 10%;\n    - V1 migration and ecosystem compatibility: 10%;\n    - Runtime and tooling coherence: 10%;\n    - demonstrated adoption potential: 8%;\n    - implementation and long-term maintenance burden: 8%;\n    - interoperability and composability: 7%.\n\n    Provide:\n\n    - raw scores;\n    - weighted scores;\n    - evidence;\n    - uncertainty;\n    - disputed assumptions;\n    - sensitivity analysis;\n    - and the conditions under which another option would become preferable.\n\n    Do not let a high feature count compensate for loss of formal assurance or\n    operational executability.\n  </decision_scorecard>\n\n  <required_experiments>\n    At minimum, perform or specify reproducibly:\n\n    1. V1-versus-V2 implementation of the representative contract suite.\n    2. A recurring-payment contract with 10, 100, and 1,000 periods, comparing\n       unrolling and bounded iteration.\n    3. A high-participant close and payout benchmark.\n    4. A WhenAll or action-set concurrency test covering ordering, partial\n       submission, atomicity, and timeout.\n    5. A typed-value experiment demonstrating unit and token errors caught\n       before deployment.\n    6. A state-compression benchmark measuring datum, transaction, execution,\n       storage, and proof impacts.\n    7. A Merkleized-continuation availability failure exercise.\n    8. An invalid-initial-state exercise.\n    9. A role-token monetary-policy attack and recovery analysis.\n    10. An oracle staleness, replay, and equivocation exercise.\n    11. A Runtime-compromise exercise in which the client verifies or rejects a\n        maliciously constructed transaction.\n    12. Differential semantics tests across every maintained implementation.\n    13. A V1-to-V2 migration dry run on representative deployed contracts.\n    14. A small user study comparing V1, the candidate V2 surface language,\n        TypeScript construction, and Blockly.\n    15. A proof-effort prototype for the two or three highest-risk proposed\n        language constructs.\n  </required_experiments>\n\n  <required_deliverables>\n\n    <deliverable_1_executive_decision>\n      A concise recommendation stating:\n\n      - Marlowe's enduring purpose;\n      - the recommended architecture;\n      - exact V2.0 scope;\n      - exact V2.1 scope;\n      - deferred and rejected proposals;\n      - migration strategy;\n      - primary target users;\n      - required funding and team;\n      - and the three largest unresolved risks.\n    </deliverable_1_executive_decision>\n\n    <deliverable_2_evidence_inventory>\n      A source, repository, version, deployment, and status inventory with\n      contradictions and documentation drift highlighted.\n    </deliverable_2_evidence_inventory>\n\n    <deliverable_3_current_architecture>\n      A complete V1 language and platform architecture, lifecycle, data-flow,\n      trust-boundary, and repository-dependency model.\n    </deliverable_3_current_architecture>\n\n    <deliverable_4_language_and_semantics_dossier>\n      A precise account of current syntax, semantics, serialization,\n      authorization, time, value, state, analysis, warnings, and errors.\n    </deliverable_4_language_and_semantics_dossier>\n\n    <deliverable_5_formal_assurance_matrix>\n      The theorem, assumption, implementation-correspondence, and proof-gap\n      matrix.\n    </deliverable_5_formal_assurance_matrix>\n\n    <deliverable_6_empirical_benchmark>\n      Reproducible contract, cost, size, transaction, usability, and failure\n      benchmarks using current protocol parameters.\n    </deliverable_6_empirical_benchmark>\n\n    <deliverable_7_v2_proposal_audit>\n      A proposal-by-proposal disposition table for all existing Marlowe V2\n      ideas.\n    </deliverable_7_v2_proposal_audit>\n\n    <deliverable_8_comparative_analysis>\n      A comparison that extracts transferable language, verification,\n      transaction-composition, runtime, and tooling patterns from other systems.\n    </deliverable_8_comparative_analysis>\n\n    <deliverable_9_architecture_options>\n      Full descriptions and weighted scorecards for the stabilization,\n      conservative V2, verified-core-plus-surface-language, composable-protocol,\n      and portable/L2 alternatives.\n    </deliverable_9_architecture_options>\n\n    <deliverable_10_recommended_v2_specification>\n      Provide a preliminary but concrete design containing:\n\n      - core and surface grammars;\n      - type and kind system;\n      - elaboration rules;\n      - operational semantics;\n      - resource-bound model;\n      - serialization;\n      - version identifiers;\n      - extension boundaries;\n      - and formal proof obligations.\n\n      Include before-and-after examples for at least eight representative\n      contracts.\n    </deliverable_10_recommended_v2_specification>\n\n    <deliverable_11_platform_architecture>\n      Specify validators, Runtime, API, SDK, authoring tools, continuation\n      storage, explorer, oracle integration, and deployment architecture.\n    </deliverable_11_platform_architecture>\n\n    <deliverable_12_security_plan>\n      End-to-end threat model, trusted-computing-base analysis, audit boundaries,\n      secure-development process, and incident-response requirements.\n    </deliverable_12_security_plan>\n\n    <deliverable_13_verification_and_test_plan>\n      Normative-specification decision, proof roadmap, conformance suite,\n      differential-test plan, fuzzing strategy, cost gates, and release gates.\n    </deliverable_13_verification_and_test_plan>\n\n    <deliverable_14_migration_plan>\n      V1/V2 coexistence, translator, equivalence checking, API migration,\n      validator registry, explorer support, deprecation, rollback, and\n      long-term-support policy.\n    </deliverable_14_migration_plan>\n\n    <deliverable_15_repository_and_governance_plan>\n      Target repository structure, code ownership, release train, semantic\n      versioning policy, MIP improvements, specification authority, audit\n      process, and maintenance model.\n    </deliverable_15_repository_and_governance_plan>\n\n    <deliverable_16_roadmap>\n      Provide:\n\n      - first 90 days;\n      - months 4 through 6;\n      - months 7 through 12;\n      - months 13 through 24;\n      - and later work.\n\n      For each phase specify:\n\n      - deliverables;\n      - dependencies;\n      - staffing;\n      - cost range;\n      - validation gates;\n      - adoption experiments;\n      - security reviews;\n      - go/no-go criteria;\n      - and rollback or scope-reduction options.\n    </deliverable_16_roadmap>\n\n    <deliverable_17_draft_improvement_proposals>\n      Outline the MIPs or equivalent proposals required for:\n\n      - language versioning;\n      - V2 core semantics;\n      - type system;\n      - serialization;\n      - validator versioning;\n      - Runtime/API compatibility;\n      - extension or composition mechanisms;\n      - migration;\n      - and conformance testing.\n    </deliverable_17_draft_improvement_proposals>\n\n    <deliverable_18_discussion_packet>\n      Produce a stakeholder workshop packet containing:\n\n      - a ten-page maximum pre-read;\n      - terminology and system map;\n      - disputed assumptions;\n      - six to ten explicit decision motions;\n      - evidence for and against each motion;\n      - stakeholder-specific questions;\n      - a two-session discussion agenda;\n      - decision matrix;\n      - unresolved-issue register;\n      - and decision-record template.\n\n      Include motions addressing at least:\n\n      1. whether Marlowe remains a strictly finite DSL;\n      2. whether V2 adopts a small verified core plus typed surface language;\n      3. the exact V2.0 feature boundary;\n      4. bounded iteration;\n      5. state compression and closing behavior;\n      6. action-set semantics;\n      7. external-script and minting composition;\n      8. the normative formal specification;\n      9. V1 compatibility;\n      10. and Marlowe's primary target user and product position.\n    </deliverable_18_discussion_packet>\n\n  </required_deliverables>\n\n  <release_gates>\n    Propose numeric gates, but at minimum require:\n\n    - no unresolved ambiguity in normative semantics;\n    - explicit resource and lifetime bounds for every deployable contract;\n    - type safety for the new source language;\n    - conformance among maintained implementations;\n    - deterministic canonical serialization;\n    - reproduced V1 behavior where compatibility is claimed;\n    - no critical or high-severity unresolved audit findings;\n    - cost regression thresholds;\n    - tests for live ledger limits and representative wallet states;\n    - complete Runtime transaction-verification guidance;\n    - migration rehearsal;\n    - documentation synchronized to release artifacts;\n    - and at least two non-toy applications or committed pilot users exercising\n      the principal V2 capabilities.\n  </release_gates>\n\n  <red_team_requirements>\n    Challenge all of the following assumptions:\n\n    - that greater expressiveness necessarily increases adoption;\n    - that non-programmers want to author executable financial contracts;\n    - that current formal proofs cover the deployed system;\n    - that bounded loops are easy to add without weakening predictability;\n    - that state compression solves rather than relocates scalability problems;\n    - that Merkleization is safe without a continuation-availability protocol;\n    - that role tokens are the correct long-term authorization abstraction;\n    - that a universal validator is always preferable;\n    - that arbitrary Plutus composition can coexist with Marlowe guarantees;\n    - that blockchain-agnostic semantics create practical multi-chain\n      portability;\n    - that a Runtime can remain both convenient and trust-minimized;\n    - that the existing V2 categories are correctly prioritized;\n    - that historical documentation reflects current code or protocol limits;\n    - and that technical modernization is justified without evidence of users.\n\n    Include the strongest case for not performing a major language redesign.\n  </red_team_requirements>\n\n  <quality_bar>\n    The final report must be repository-specific, evidence-driven, and\n    implementable.\n\n    Do not produce:\n\n    - a generic smart-contract-language survey;\n    - a generic Cardano overview;\n    - an unranked feature wishlist;\n    - a proposal to make Marlowe Turing complete;\n    - a redesign that discards the existing semantics and ecosystem without\n      quantifying migration cost;\n    - unsupported claims about safety or adoption;\n    - hard-coded ledger limits copied from historical documentation;\n    - or an architecture in which the LLM, Runtime, oracle, or external script\n      silently becomes trusted.\n\n    Use exact dates, versions, commit hashes, script hashes, benchmark\n    configurations, and confidence labels.\n\n    Label inference as inference. Show disagreements among sources. Quantify\n    uncertainty. Make tradeoffs explicit.\n\n    The report should conclude with a concrete decision, not merely a menu of\n    possibilities.\n  </quality_bar>\n\n  <final_required_answer>\n    End with a direct recommendation in this form:\n\n    1. Preserve:\n       the exact Marlowe principles and V1 constructs that should remain.\n\n    2. Change in V2.0:\n       the smallest coherent, formally defensible, deployable modernization.\n\n    3. Develop for V2.1:\n       capabilities requiring further semantics, proofs, experiments, or\n       infrastructure.\n\n    4. Move outside the core:\n       features better implemented in the surface language, libraries, Runtime,\n       oracle protocol, transaction-composition layer, or applications.\n\n    5. Reject:\n       proposals whose safety, complexity, migration, cost, or demand tradeoff\n       is unfavorable.\n\n    6. Prove and test:\n       the exact formal, security, conformance, and empirical work required\n       before release.\n\n    7. Migrate:\n       the exact V1/V2 coexistence and compatibility strategy.\n\n    8. Build:\n       the 90-day, 12-month, and 24-month implementation sequence.\n\n    9. Govern:\n       the specification, repository, release, audit, and MIP process.\n\n    10. Validate demand:\n        the pilots and adoption evidence required to justify continued\n        investment.\n  </final_required_answer>\n\n</deep_research_assignment>\n27:  ZKIR, Runtime, and trust boundaries.\n36:## K Framework and ZKIR semantics\n38:Started 2026-09-03 on branch `zkir-k-semantics`. Goal: an executable K\n39:Framework semantics of ZKIR v3. Pins: K v7.1.337 (`4a46d123`), arc-zkir\n41:surface, standalone `midnight-zkir` `2ffe2d17`.\n43:- [ZKIR semantics in K: plan](zkir-k-semantics-plan.md) — the synthesis:\n46:- [The ZKIR semantics in K, as built](zkir/zkir-k-definition.md) — module\n49:- [K Framework overview](k-framework/k-framework-overview.md) — what K is,\n66:- [ZKIR instruction set](zkir/zkir-instruction-set.md) — program structure,\n69:- [ZKIR types and values](zkir/zkir-type-system.md) — BLS12-381 scalar\n71:- [ZKIR VM operational semantics](zkir/zkir-vm-semantics.md) — state,\n74:- [Compact to ZKIR pipeline](zkir/compact-to-zkir-pipeline.md) — how\n77:- [ZKIR formal specification and Agda mechanization](zkir/zkir-formal-spec-agda.md)\n78:  — arc-zkir pins, formal model, trust base, theorems, module map, timeline.\n79:- [ZKIR v3 in-circuit versus off-circuit divergences](zkir/zkir-v3-divergence-review.md)\n81:- [Midnight's K tooling](zkir/midnight-k-tooling.md) — k-rust and\n## Subagent dispatch requires multi-agent support\n\nAdd to your Codex config (`~/.codex/config.toml`):\n\n```toml\n[features]\nmulti_agent = true\n```\n\nThis enables `spawn_agent`, `wait_agent`, and `close_agent` for skills like `dispatching-parallel-agents` and `subagent-driven-development`. When using subagent-driven-development, you should always close implementer and reviewer subagents when they have finished all their work.\n\n## Environment Detection\n\nSkills that create worktrees or finish branches should detect their\nenvironment with read-only git commands before proceeding:\n\n```bash\nGIT_DIR=$(cd \"$(git rev-parse --git-dir)\" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)\nGIT_COMMON=$(cd \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)\" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)\nBRANCH=$(git branch --show-current)\n```\n\n- `GIT_DIR != GIT_COMMON` → already in a linked worktree (skip creation)\n- `BRANCH` empty → detached HEAD (cannot branch/push/PR from sandbox)\n\nSee `using-git-worktrees` Step 0 and `finishing-a-development-branch`\nStep 1 for how each skill uses these signals.\n\n## Codex App Finishing\n\nWhen the sandbox blocks branch/push operations (detached HEAD in an\nexternally managed worktree), the agent commits all work and informs\nthe user to use the App's native controls:\n\n- **\"Create branch\"** — names the branch, then commit/push/PR via App UI\n- **\"Hand off to local\"** — transfers work to the user's local checkout\n\nThe agent can still run tests, stage files, and output suggested branch\nnames, commit messages, and PR descriptions for the user to copy.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
