 -> dict[str, Any]:\n   272\t        job = KApply('genJob' if gen else 'job', [program, preimage_term(preimage)])\n   273\t        kore = self.krun.kast_to_kore(job, KSort('Job'))\n   274\t        res = self.krun.run_process(kore, depth=depth)\n   275\t        if res.returncode != 0:\n   276\t            return {'status': 'krun-failed', 'error': res.stderr[-2000:]}\n   277\t        cfg = self.krun.kore_to_kast(KoreParser(res.stdout).pattern())\n   278\t        status = find_cell(cfg, '<status>')\n   279\t        assert isinstance(status, KApply)\n   280\t        out: dict[str, Any] = {}\n   281\t        if status.label.name == 'ok':\n   282\t            out['status'] = 'ok'\n   283\t        else:\n   284\t            out['status'] = 'error'\n   285\t            out['error'] = tok_str(status.args[0])\n   286\t        memory = {}\n   287\t        for k, v in map_items(find_cell(cfg, '<mem>')):\n   288\t            variant, enc = encode_value(v, self.ext)\n   289\t            memory[tok_str(k)] = {'variant': variant, 'encoded': [str(e) for e in enc]}\n   290\t        out['memory'] = memory\n   291\t        out['pis'] = [str(tok_int(x)) for x in list_items(find_cell(cfg, '<pi>'))]\n   292\t        out['pi_skips'] = [skip(x) for x in list_items(find_cell(cfg, '<skips>'))]\n   293\t        out['cursors'] = (tok_int(find_cell(cfg, '<pubInIdx>')), tok_int(find_cell(cfg, '<pubOutIdx>')), tok_int(find_cell(cfg, '<privIdx>')))\n   294\t        out['constraints'] = len(list_items(find_cell(cfg, '<constraints>')))\n   295\t        out['k_cell'] = self.krun.pretty_print(find_cell(cfg, '<k>')).strip()\n   296\t        out['needs'] = [need(x) for x in list_items(find_cell(cfg, '<needs>'))]\n   297\t        verdicts = list_items(find_cell(cfg, '<verdicts>'))\n   298\t        bad = []\n   299\t        for v in verdicts:\n   300\t            assert isinstance(v, KApply) and v.label.name == 'verdict'\n   301\t            outcome = v.args[1]\n   302\t            assert isinstance(outcome, KApply)\n   303\t            if outcome.label.name != 'holds':\n   304\t                gate = self.krun.pretty_print(v.args[0]).replace('\\n', ' ')\n   305\t                gate = ' '.join(gate.split())\n   306\t                bad.append((outcome.label.name, tok_str(outcome.args[0]) if outcome.args else '', gate[:120]))\n   307\t        out['verdicts'] = len(verdicts)\n   308\t        out['violations'] = bad\n   309\t        return out\n   310\t\n   311\t    def run_file(self, path: Path, preimage: dict[str, Any], gen: bool = False) -> dict[str, Any]:\n   312\t        return self.run(zkir_kast.load_program(path, ext=self.ext), preimage, gen=gen)\n   313\t\n   314\t\n   315\tdef main() -> int:\n   316\t    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n   317\t    ap.add_argument('file', type=Path)\n   318\t    ap.add_argument('preimage', type=Path)\n   319\t    ap.add_argument('--definition', type=Path, default=None)\n   320\t    ap.add_argument('--ext', action='store_true')\n   321\t    args = ap.parse_args()\n   322\t    runner = Runner(args.definition, ext=args.ext)\n   323\t    with open(args.preimage) as f:\n   324\t        pre = json.load(f)\n   325\t    print(json.dumps(runner.run_file(args.file, pre), indent=1))\n   326\t    return 0\n   327\t\n   328\t\n   329\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   330\t    sys.exit(main())\nPASS  f02_assert_non_boolean                     Finding 2            K=error    Rust=error  gate=assert:holds\n        off-circuit demands cond == 1, the circuit only cond != 0\n        K: Expected boolean, found: 2 | Rust: Expected boolean, found: 02\nPASS  f03_guard_uncoupled                        Finding 3            K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=public_input:holds\n        an inactive guard yields the default value off-circuit; the gate never mentions the guard\n        K: ok | Rust: ok\nPASS  f04_less_than_odd_bits                     Finding 4            K=error    Rust=error  gate=less_than:unknown\n        a = 8 exceeds 3 bits off-circuit; the chip would accept the padded 4-bit bound (output absent, so unknown)\n        K: unknown: register %lt is not in the witness | Rust: Bit bound failed: 08 is not 3-bit\nPASS  f05_noncanonical_foreign_limbs             Finding 5            K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=add:holds\n        limbs encoding p + 5 decode (reduced) as 5 on both sides\n        K: ok | Rust: ok\nPASS  f06_cond_select_bytes32                    Finding 6            K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=cond_select:synthErr\n        cond_select on Bytes32 is accepted off-circuit but has no in-circuit arm\n        K: synthErr: Unsupported cond_select: Bytes32 ? Bytes32 | Rust: ok\nPASS  f07_constrain_eq_jubjub_scalar             Finding 7            K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=constrain_eq:synthErr\n        constrain_eq on JubjubScalar is accepted off-circuit but has no in-circuit arm\n        K: synthErr: Unsupported constrain_eq: JubjubScalar == JubjubScalar | Rust: ok\nPASS  f08_bytes32_from_low_high_foreign_high     Finding 8            K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=bytes32_from_low_high:synthErr\n        a foreign high operand is accepted off-circuit, the circuit requires Native\n        K: synthErr: bytes32_from_low_high high: cannot convert Secp256k1Base to Native | Rust: ok\nPASS  f10_reconstitute_bits_256                  Finding 10           K=wfError  Rust=error  gate=reconstitute_field:n/a\n        bits >= 256 is rejected statically (Rust off-circuit: Excessive bit count; keygen would underflow)\n        K: wfError ( \"reconstitute_field: excessive bit count\" ) | Rust: Excessive bit count\nPASS  f11_curve25519_torsion_point               Finding 11           K=error    Rust=error  gate=from_coordinates:violated\n        (0, -1) has order 2: on the curve, not in the subgroup; off-circuit both sides error, in circuit the cofactor-cleared point is unsatisfiable\n        K: violated: from_coordinates: point is not in the prime-order subgroup | Rust: Cannot build a Curve25519Point point from (0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000\nPASS  f12_ec_mul_generator_p256                  Finding 12           K=error    Rust=error  gate=ec_mul_generator:synthErr\n        no generator for secp256r1 scalars on either side\n        K: synthErr: ec_mul_generator output: Unsupported EcMulGenerator for scalar of type Secp256r1Scalar | Rust: Unsupported EcMulGenerator for scalar of type Secp256r1Scalar\nPASS  f13_chip_gating_from_bytes32               Finding 13           K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=from_bytes32:synthErr\n        the secp256k1 chip is never initialised (used_chips ignores from_bytes32)\n        K: synthErr: chip not initialised for Secp256k1Base | Rust: ok\nPASS  k01_jubjub_from_coordinates_parity_only    K1 (new)             K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=from_coordinates:violated\n        off-circuit only the parity of x is used (decompression), so a wrong x yields the real point; in-circuit (x, y) must lie on the curve\n        K: violated: from_coordinates: (x, y) is not on the curve | Rust: ok\nPASS  f01_reconstitute_overflow                  Finding 1 (retired)  K=error    Rust=error  gate=reconstitute_field:unknown\n        divisor * 2^8 + modulus >= r: off-circuit overflow error; in circuit the sum would wrap mod r\n        K: unknown: register %o is not in the witness | Rust: Reconstituted element overflows field\n\n13/13 divergence cases behave as expected\n20:ZKIR v3 is a straight-line, single-assignment intermediate representation over the BLS12-381 scalar field, with named registers, no loops and no branches; conditional behaviour is expressed with branch-free `cond_select` and guarded transcript instructions (`impact`, `public_input`, `private_input`) (CLM-0700; [zkir-vm-semantics.md](zkir/zkir-vm-semantics.md) and [zkir-instruction-set.md](zkir/zkir-instruction-set.md); source fact; not reproduced; high; S5). Every instruction has two semantics that must agree: an off-circuit witness computation (`preprocess`) that fills registers and builds the public-input vector, and an in-circuit constraint synthesis that emits Halo2 PLONKish gates over the same registers (CLM-0701; SRC-0025 docs/zkir-v3-spec.md; source fact; not reproduced; high; S4). The arc-zkir Agda development proves that the two agree (circuit faithfulness, statement soundness, extraction uniqueness) for the 34-instruction, 13-type surface at `midnight-ledger` `ledger-9` commit `92e8bdd3` (CLM-0702; [zkir-formal-spec-agda.md](zkir/zkir-formal-spec-agda.md); source fact; not reproduced; high; S4). The standalone `midnight-zkir` repository has since grown to 42 instructions and 15 types, which the mechanization does not yet cover (CLM-0703; [contradictions.md](contradictions.md); repository observation; reproduced; high; S5).\n24:The definition is one K definition with one configuration that carries both semantics at once, so that every instruction rule updates the witness store and appends to the constraint set in the same rewrite (CLM-0704; inference; high; S2). This mirrors the Agda model's paired `preprocess` and `synth` functions and makes the faithfulness property a checkable statement inside K: after a run, every emitted constraint must evaluate to true under the witness store (CLM-0704). Splitting witness and constraint semantics into two definitions was rejected because the divergence review shows that the interesting bugs are exactly the places where the two disagree, and a single configuration makes each disagreement a visible, testable difference between two cells of the same state (CLM-0705; [zkir-v3-divergence-review.md](zkir/zkir-v3-divergence-review.md); inference; high; S2). A mode cell that switches rules on and off was rejected because it multiplies rules without adding information; constraint emission is cheap data and can always be on, and \"check mode\" is a function over the final state rather than a third semantics (CLM-0705).\n26:One refinement follows from the advisory consult recorded in `raw/notes/zkir-k-plan-advisory-2026-09-03.md`: constraint emission must not depend on the witness computation succeeding. A witness error (failed assertion, transcript mismatch, undefined register) sets the `status` cell and freezes the witness cells, but the synthesis half of every remaining rule still fires, so the constraint set at the end of a run is always the constraint set of the whole circuit, exactly as the real synthesiser produces it. This is what makes the review's findings 2 and 3 testable: a circuit whose preprocess halts but whose constraints all pass, or an inactive guard whose registers may take arbitrary values, both show up as a disagreement between `status` and the constraint check rather than being masked by an early stop (CLM-0720; raw/notes/zkir-k-plan-advisory-2026-09-03.md and [zkir-v3-divergence-review.md](zkir/zkir-v3-divergence-review.md); inference; high; S2).\n40:    <constraints> .List </constraints>   // emitted algebraic constraints\n61:The definition is written for canonical K v7.1.337 at commit `4a46d123` and executed with the LLVM backend for concrete runs and the Haskell backend for claims (CLM-0713; [k-framework-overview.md](k-framework/k-framework-overview.md); inference; high; S6). That is the same K commit that Midnight's `k-rust` validates against, so compatibility with `k-rust` is a stated goal but not a day-one gate: the definition avoids features that `k-rust` documents as unsupported where the cost is zero, and a `k-rust` run is a later milestone (CLM-0714; [midnight-k-tooling.md](zkir/midnight-k-tooling.md); inference; medium; S4). K is not installed on this machine yet; the install route is `kup`, which requires Nix on WSL2 (CLM-0715; [k-framework-overview.md](k-framework/k-framework-overview.md); source fact; not reproduced; high; S6).\n65:Three oracles, in increasing strength (CLM-0716; inference; high; S2):\n68:2. Constraint satisfaction: after each successful K run, every entry of the `constraints` cell must hold under the `mem` cell. Failures are candidates for the divergence list, and the 13 known divergences are the first negative tests.\n69:3. Correspondence with the Agda model: the Agda `preprocess` and `synth` functions can be run on the same programs once the Nix toolchain in arc-zkir is installed, giving a third independent trace. This is the only oracle that speaks to the theorems, and it has not been run.\n77:3. Module `ZKIR-FIELD` and `ZKIR-TYPES`: field arithmetic, curve constructors, Bytes32 conversions with unit claims for inverse and encode/decode round trips. Done 2026-09-05 as `zkir-field.k` (modular arithmetic, Tonelli-Shanks), `zkir-curves.k` (affine Jubjub, Curve25519, secp256k1, secp256r1; Jubjub decompression as midnight-circuits `from_xy` does it), `zkir-values.k` (the 13 value constructors, `encode_offcircuit` and `from_public_input` encodings including the 64-bit and 51-bit limb schemes of the foreign fields), `zkir-hash.k` (Poseidon t=3 with the pasta-hadeshash constants, hash-to-curve by Shallue-van de Woestijne, SHA-256, Keccak-256) and `zkir-ops.k` (one function per `*_offcircuit`). `tools/unit_values.py` checks 41 field, curve, decompression and encoding cases against an independent Python implementation and `tools/unit_hash.py` checks 18 hash cases against the Rust crate itself (CLM-0726; evidence/zkir-k-unit-values-2026-09-05.txt and evidence/zkir-k-unit-hash-2026-09-05.txt; executed test; reproduced; high; S1). The Rust reference is `zkir-oracle`, a 120-line harness in a worktree of `midnight-ledger` at 92e8bdd3 (`repos/_build/ledger-92e8bdd3/zkir-oracle`) that calls the crate's `preprocess` (made `pub` by a one-line patch) and prints memory, public inputs and skips as JSON (CLM-0727; repos/_build/ledger-92e8bdd3/zkir-oracle/src/main.rs; repository observation; reproduced; high; S1).\n78:4. Module `ZKIR-VM`: the 34 instruction rules, each updating `mem`, `pi`, `skips`, cursors and `constraints`. Test: oracle 1 over the precompile corpus. Done 2026-09-05 as `zkir-vm.k`: the configuration of the plan (`<mem>`, `<pi>`, `<skips>`, three cursors, `<outputs>`, `<constraints>`, `<status>`), every instruction emitting its gate before its witness half runs, the witness frozen after the first error while gates keep being emitted, transcript exhaustion and the communications commitment checked at the end, and a generation mode that the harness uses to discover which transcript values a program consumes. `tools/diff_test.py` runs every version-3 program of the corpus (43 crate tests seeded with the crate's own literal inputs, 6 micro-dao precompiles, 7 escrow and swap artifacts, 9 handmade programs) through K and through `zkir-oracle` on the same preimages, with up to eight attempts per program and one perturbed preimage after each success: 314 comparisons, 314 agree on status, every register's variant and encoding, the public-input vector and the skip vector; 37 comparisons are successful witness runs and the rest compare error paths (CLM-0728; evidence/zkir-k-differential-92e8bdd3-2026-09-05.txt; executed test; reproduced; high; S1).\n79:5. Constraint checker and the 13 divergence tests (oracle 2). Done 2026-09-05 as `zkir-constraints.k`: each gate is evaluated against the final witness as the in-circuit relation read off `Relation::circuit` and the `*_incircuit` functions, with four outcomes (holds, violated, synthesis error for a missing dispatch arm or an uninitialised chip per `used_chips`, unknown when the witness stopped before the register existed). On every successful differential run all gates hold (CLM-0729; evidence/zkir-k-differential-92e8bdd3-2026-09-05.txt; executed test; reproduced; high; S1). `tools/divergence_tests.py` encodes the review's findings 1 to 8 and 10 to 13 as programs with preimages and checks the off-circuit status on both sides and the gate outcome: 13 of 13 behave as the review describes, for example `assert` of 2 fails off-circuit while its gate holds, `cond_select` on Bytes32 succeeds off-circuit while its gate is a synthesis error, and a program touching secp256k1 only through `from_bytes32` succeeds off-circuit while its gate reports an uninitialised chip (CLM-0730; evidence/zkir-k-divergence-tests-2026-09-05.txt; executed test; reproduced; high; S1). The thirteenth case is new: Jubjub `from_coordinates` off-circuit uses only the parity of `x`, so `(x+2, y)` yields the true point on both sides while the exact-coordinate gate is violated; recorded in [contradictions.md](contradictions.md).\n80:6. `ZKIR-EXT` module for the `2ffe2d17` surface; `k-rust` compatibility run; Agda correspondence run (oracle 3). Done 2026-09-05 in three parts. `zkir-ext.k` adds the types Bool, Byte and Bytes<n>, the nine instructions `and`, `or`, `xor`, `concat`, `slice`, `nth`, `reverse`, `load_constant` and `sha512`, logical `neg` on Bool, and the canonical-form check that 2ffe2d1 performs on every decoded value; it is compiled as a second main module and its programs are checked against a second oracle built from midnight-zkir at 2ffe2d1: 61 crate test programs, the six micro-dao precompiles and the handmade programs give 366 comparisons, 366 agreeing, 40 successful runs, no non-holding gate (CLM-0737; evidence/zkir-k-differential-ext-2ffe2d1-2026-09-05.txt; executed test; reproduced; high; S1). The extension run surfaced a second new finding, K2: both crates index the transcript slice directly, so an unguarded `public_input` or `private_input` with a transcript that is too short panics with an index-out-of-range instead of returning an error, while K reports an error (CLM-0738; evidence/zkir-k-divergence-tests-2026-09-05.txt case `k02`; executed test; reproduced; high; S1). The arc-zkir v3 Agda development type-checks in full but has no concrete `Assumptions` instance, so oracle 3 cannot be run (CLM-0736). `k-rust` 0.4.0 parses the definition but does not finish compiling it within 25 minutes, where canonical K needs twenty seconds (CLM-0740; evidence/k-rust-compatibility-2026-09-05.txt; executed test; reproduced; high; S1).\n84:The largest risk is that the crate and the spec disagree on an instruction and the K definition silently follows one of them; the mitigation is oracle 2 plus the divergence list as explicit negative tests (CLM-0718; inference; high; S2). The second risk is chip gating: non-native operations are only constrained when their chip is initialised, so a K constraint set that always emits constraints will be stricter than the real circuit in exactly the cases the divergence review calls unconstrained; those cases must be modelled as an explicit `used_chips` cell rather than papered over (CLM-0719; [zkir-v3-divergence-review.md](zkir/zkir-v3-divergence-review.md); inference; high; S2).\n86:Two further risks come from the advisory consult (CLM-0721; raw/notes/zkir-k-plan-advisory-2026-09-03.md; recommendation; not reproduced; high; S2). First, symbolic execution with the Haskell backend will not decide non-linear modular field arithmetic or hash functions; claims over the field need `simplification` lemmas, and hashes must stay uninterpreted function symbols in proofs. Second, oracle 2 only establishes completeness, that the honest witness satisfies the emitted constraints; it cannot show that no other witness does, so the K definition must not be described as proving soundness or the absence of underconstrained circuits. Soundness remains the arc-zkir theorems' territory, and the K work feeds them test programs, not proofs.\n90:The five decisions above were reviewed on 2026-09-03 by an advisory consult that agreed with the surface pin, the field representation and the compatibility stance, and disagreed with coupled constraint emission and in-K JSON parsing; both disagreements were accepted and are reflected in the text. The consult ran on `agy` with `gemini-3.8-flash-high` because the configured Claude advisor failed with API overload errors; the verbatim verdict is preserved in `raw/notes/zkir-k-plan-advisory-2026-09-03.md` (CLM-0722; raw/notes/zkir-k-plan-advisory-2026-09-03.md; repository observation; reproduced; high; S2).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
