to a\n534:                      do_communications_commitment : 𝔹,\n538:  | Encode             input : Operand,  outputs : [ Identifier ]\n545:  | Impact             guard : Operand, inputs : [ Operand ]\n556:  | DivModPowerOfTwo   val : Operand, bits : ℕ, outputs : [ Identifier ]\n594:- **WF1 — input arity / encoding.** The total encoded width of `inputs`,\n595:  `Σᵢ encoded_len(inputs[i].type)`, equals `|P.inputs|` at runtime (checked in\n600:  - `DivModPowerOfTwo { bits }`, `ReconstituteField { bits }`:\n602:- **WF3 — arities.** `Encode` requires `|outputs| = encoded_len(type of input)`;\n603:  `DivModPowerOfTwo` requires exactly 2 outputs; `Output` requires\n614:  the intended discipline is single assignment.\n615:- **WF5 — `Impact` discipline.** Each `Impact` declares its own group of\n643:- `encode` / `decode` — §4.\n649:1. Decode inputs: walking `S.inputs` in order, consume `encoded_len(type)`\n652:2. Seed `π = [P.binding_input]`. If `S.do_communications_commitment` is set,\n653:   require `P.communications_commitment = some (c, _)` and append `c` to `π` (so\n654:   the commitment is `π[1]`), failing if the preimage carries none. If the flag\n655:   is clear, `π` stays `[P.binding_input]` and `P.communications_commitment` is\n667:| `Encode(in, outs)` | `\\|outs\\| = encoded_len(type in)` | `outs ↦ encode(in)` | — |\n131:    -- affine coordinates on the Edwards model); `fromCoordsJ` is partial\n134:    -- `fromCoordsJ` is also the *in-circuit* contract of\n142:    fromCoordsJ : Fr → Fr → Maybe JubjubPoint\n144:    -- Scalar encoding (canonical 252-bit) and the Native → JubjubScalar\n345:    -- `fromCoordsC` is partial like `fromCoordsJ`: the coordinates\n349:    -- note on `fromCoordsJ` above and spec §9). Unlike the Weierstrass\n479:    coordsJ-fromCoordsJ : ∀ p → uncurry fromCoordsJ (coordsJ p) ≡ just p\n480:    fromCoordsJ-coordsJ : ∀ {x y p}\n481:      → fromCoordsJ x y ≡ just p → coordsJ p ≡ (x , y)\n    62\t## Summary\n    63\t\n    64\t| Finding | Assessment | Root cause | Blast radius | Status | Suggested action |\n    65\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    66\t| 1. Retired | — | — | — | — | — |\n    67\t| 2. Retired | — | — | — | — | — |\n    68\t| 3. PublicInput/PrivateInput guard–value coupling | by design | something else — by design, not a gap | n/a | N/A (by design) | document for producers |\n    69\t| 4. Retired | — | — | — | — | — |\n    70\t| 5. Secp256k1 decode accepts non-canonical limb encodings | completeness gap | global assumption violation | **prover** (panic half) / **dev** (canonicity half) | **Fixed**: panic half (circuits 7.2.4). Open: canonicity half | reject non-canonical input; validate flag |\n    71\t| 6. CondSelect accepts Bytes32/JubjubScalar off-circuit, can't build in-circuit | completeness gap (preprocess vs circuit) | preprocess/circuit mismatch — preprocess accepts more | **dev** | Open | align the type lists (off-circuit and in-circuit) |\n    72\t| 7. ConstrainEq accepts JubjubScalar off-circuit, can't build in-circuit | completeness gap (preprocess vs circuit) | preprocess/circuit mismatch — preprocess accepts more | **dev** | Open | align the type lists (off-circuit and in-circuit) |\n    73\t| 8. Bytes32FromLowHigh: `low`/`high` type lists disagree (Rust-internal) | Rust-internal inconsistency | something else — doesn't map cleanly onto the other three | **dev** | Open | settle the intended `high` type list; reject non-Native off-circuit too |\n    74\t| 9. Stale doc comments (Encode widths, TestEq type list) | cosmetic, no functional effect | something else — cosmetic | n/a | **Fixed**: Encode widths. Open: TestEq list | doc-only PR upstream |\n    75\t| 10. ReconstituteField: unguarded bit-count subtraction in-circuit | malformed-source robustness (keygen/synthesis) | unresolved — flagged, not classified (see below) | **prover** (keygen panic/OOM) | Open | mirror the preprocess `bits` guard in the circuit arm |\n    76\t| 11. Curve25519: off-subgroup input panics (decode fixed; FromCoordinates hint open) | panic instead of error; not live today | global assumption violation | **prover** | **Fixed**: decode half (circuits 7.2.4). Open: witness-hint half | switch the FromCoordinates hint to `try_into_subgroup` |\n    77\t| 12. EcMulGenerator not extended to Secp256r1/Curve25519 | parity gap, not a ZK-completeness issue — intent unconfirmed | something else — not a divergence | n/a | N/A (not a divergence) | confirm intent; add arms or document |\n    78\t| 13. Chip gating misses FromBytes32-only foreign-field use | prover panics at keygen; not live today | something else — Rust-internal/architectural | **prover** | Open | add FromBytes32's `val_t` to `used_chips` |\n    79\t\n    80\tFindings 1, 2, and 4 are retired. Of the remaining 10\n    81\tfindings: 2 (findings 3, 12) are N/A — not real divergences (by design\n    82\t/ a completeness asymmetry, not a bug); of the remaining 8, 5 are fully\n    83\topen (6, 7, 8, 10, 13), 3 are partially fixed (5, 9, 11 — one half\n    84\tfixed upstream, one half still open), and none are fully fixed yet.\n    85\tEverything confirmed among these is a completeness gap, a\n    86\trobustness/panic defect, or cosmetic.\n    87\t\n    88\tEverything else we audited is already enforced in-circuit — see \"Also\n    89\tchecked, found enforced\" at the end. Findings 1–5 are from an earlier,\n    90\tpartial hand-review; findings 6–10 are from a later, systematic\n    91\tlayer-2 conformance-reading pass (2026-08-05) covering all 34\n    92\tinstructions across three axes (semantics/circuit/encoding) — see\n    93\t\"Open questions from the 2026-08-05 conformance-reading pass\" after\n    94\tfinding 10 for what that pass flagged but could not resolve.\n    95\t\n    96\t**Blast radius taxonomy.** Alongside root cause (*why* the divergence\n    97\texists), the summary table also answers *who is harmed and how far it\n    98\treaches*: **network** (breaks mainnet — halt/fork/chain-wide DoS on\n    99\tthe shared consensus path, reachable by any tx/block); **contract**\n   100\t(one contract and its users — a forged proof lets the verifier accept\n   101\ta statement it shouldn't, scoping fund loss/lock to that contract's\n   102\t`VerifierKey`); **prover** (local/self-DoS — `preprocess`/synthesis\n   103\tpanics or OOMs); **dev** (the contract developer can't build/prove an\n   104\tintended program — completeness/usability, no user or network harm).\n   105\tNo finding in this document is `network`-class: a soundness gap makes\n   106\tthe verifier *accept* a forged proof, which is a valid, well-formed\n   107\ttransaction every validator processes identically — contract-scoped,\n   108\tnever a halt or fork; a robustness gap crashes the *prover*, which\n   109\tnever reaches a verifier.\n   110\t\n   111\t## 1. Retired\n   112\t\n   113\t## 2. Retired\n   114\t\n   115\t## 3. PublicInput / PrivateInput: guard and value are uncoupled in-circuit\n   116\t\n   117\t**What the circuit enforces.** Both instructions witness a fresh value and\n   118\tallocate it well-typed via `assign_incircuit` (`ir_vm.rs:999-1003`), so the\n   119\t*type* of the cell is constrained. The guard deliberately takes no part in\n   120\tthe constraints — the instruction documentation is explicit: \"the `guard`\n   121\tDOES NOT participate in in-circuit constraints\" (`ir.rs:871-872`,\n   122\t`894-895`).\n   123\t\n   124\t**What preprocess requires.** The guard is read as a bool (rejecting\n   125\tnon-`0`/`1`, `ir_vm.rs:240-251`) and branches: guard-false binds the\n   126\tdefault value for the type; guard-true decodes the next transcript element\n   127\t(`ir_vm.rs:353-365` public, `367-383` private).\n   128\t\n   129\t**The gap.** In-circuit, nothing ties the witnessed value to the guard: a\n   130\twitness may put any well-typed value in an \"inactive\" cell (where\n   131\tpreprocess would bind the default), and the guard wire itself need not be\n   132\tboolean unless constrained elsewhere. Several distinct witnesses therefore\n   133\tsatisfy the circuit for the same public inputs.\n   134\t\n   135\t**Why this is by design.** These cells are the prover's inputs — the\n   310\tBytes32, JubjubPoint, Secp256k1Point, Secp256k1Base, Secp256k1Scalar}` —\n   311\tno JubjubScalar arm; falls to `Error::Synthesis`.\n   312\t\n   313\t**What preprocess requires.** `constrain_eq_offcircuit` is\n   314\ttype-generic (checks `a.get_type() == b.get_type()`, then value\n   315\tequality), so equal-value JubjubScalar pairs succeed off-circuit — the\n   316\tsame off-circuit/in-circuit split as finding 6, on a different\n   317\tinstruction.\n   318\t\n   319\t**The gap.** A source with `ConstrainEq(a, b)` over two equal-value\n   320\tJubjubScalar cells: `preprocess` succeeds, the circuit cannot be built.\n   321\t\n   322\t**Suggested action.** Same choice as finding 6: either drop\n   323\tJubjubScalar from `constrain_eq_offcircuit`'s acceptance (matching the\n   324\tin-circuit list) or add a JubjubScalar arm in-circuit.\n   325\t\n   326\t(A model-side counterpart — our `constrain-eq` reuses `valEq?` and our\n   327\t`holds`'s `eq` clause is untyped, so the model both under- and\n   328\tover-shoots the real behavior — is not an implementation divergence and\n   329\tis tracked separately in #61, which also records the `valEq?`/TestEq\n   330\ttrap caught in review.)\n   331\t\n   332\t## 8. Bytes32FromLowHigh: `low`/`high` type lists disagree (Rust-internal)\n   333\t\n   334\t**What the circuit enforces.** `high` is forced through\n   335\t`TryInto<AssignedNative>` immediately — Native only. `low` dispatches\n   336\tthrough the same `into_bytes32_incircuit` used elsewhere, which covers\n   337\t`{Native, Secp256k1Base, Secp256k1Scalar}`.\n   338\t\n   339\t**What preprocess requires.** Both `low` and `high` go through\n   340\t`into_bytes32_offcircuit`, which also covers `{Native, Secp256k1Base,\n   341\tSecp256k1Scalar}` — so a `high` operand of type Secp256k1Base/Scalar\n   342\tcan succeed off-circuit if its value happens to fit the required bound\n   343\t(`< 256`).\n   344\t\n   345\t**The gap — Rust-internal inconsistency.** `low` and `high` are *not*\n   346\ttreated the same way in-circuit (`high` is Native-only, `low` is\n   347\tthree-type), even though they're treated identically off-circuit. A\n   348\t`high` cell of type Secp256k1Base holding a small value (e.g. `5`)\n   349\tsucceeds in `preprocess` but the circuit can't be built for it.\n   350\t\n   351\t**Suggested action.** Decide the intended type list for `high`\n   421\t## 11. Curve25519: on-curve, off-subgroup input panics (decode fixed; FromCoordinates' witness hint still open)\n   422\t\n   423\tFound while scoping Curve25519 mechanization (2026-08-06) — Curve25519\n   424\tis not covered by findings 1–10 above (they predate Curve25519's\n   425\texistence in the crate). Included here because it's the same kind of\n   426\tfinding as 1–10 (a decode-robustness gap), just discovered by a\n   427\tdifferent route (direct source reading while preparing to mechanize the\n   428\tcurve, not the systematic sweep).\n   429\t\n   430\t**~~What decode did (circuits 7.2.2).~~ Fixed at circuits 7.2.4, live at\n   431\tthis pin.** `zkir-v3/src/ir_instructions/encode.rs`'s\n   432\t`decode_offcircuit`/`decode_incircuit` for `Curve25519Point` both route\n   433\tthrough the foreign Edwards chip's `from_public_input`\n   434\t(`ecc/foreign/edwards_chip.rs:367-378`). At circuits 7.2.2 this used to\n   435\tbe `C::from_xy(x, y).map(|p| p.into_subgroup())` — `from_xy` validates\n   436\tthe twisted-Edwards curve equation (rejecting off-curve `(x, y)`,\n   437\treturning `None`, sound), but `into_subgroup()` panicked on an\n   438\ton-curve-but-off-subgroup point via `.expect(\"point must be in the\n   439\tprime-order subgroup\")`. At 7.2.4 (this pin, confirmed against\n   440\t`midnight-zk` commit `cfa6250`) the call is now\n   441\t`C::from_xy(x, y)?.try_into_subgroup()` — the fallible variant, so an\n   442\toff-subgroup point now decodes to a clean `None` instead of panicking.\n   443\tSame shared-function fix as finding 5's panic half.\n   444\t\n   445\t**Why this mattered (historical: the panic mechanism, now closed on\n   446\tthis path).** Curve25519 has cofactor 8: a random on-curve point has\n   447\tonly a 1/8 chance of already being in the prime-order subgroup.\n   448\t`from_xy`'s curve-equation check does not exclude the other 7/8 (points\n   449\twith a nonzero 8-torsion component) — those used to pass `from_xy` and\n   450\tthen hit the panicking `into_subgroup()`. Unlike finding 10's narrow,\n   451\thard-to-hit `bits == 256` case, this was the *statistically likely*\n   452\toutcome for any on-curve point that isn't specifically constructed to\n   453\tbe torsion-free, and reachable from `preprocess` itself (not just\n   454\tcircuit synthesis) — which is why it's worth recording even now that\n   455\tit's fixed.\n   456\t\n   457\t**The same panic sits on `FromCoordinates`' in-circuit witness-hint\n   458\tpath** (verified 2026-08-06, while writing the Curve25519 mechanization's\n   459\tspec sections). The two arms of `from_coordinates.rs` are asymmetric:\n   460\t\n   461\t- *Off-circuit*, `from_coordinates_offcircuit`'s Curve25519 arm\n   462\t  (`from_coordinates.rs:64-69`) is\n   463\t  `from_xy(x, y).and_then(|p| Curve25519Subgroup::from_edwards(p.0))\n   464\t  .ok_or(...)` — a graceful error on both off-curve and\n   465\t  on-curve-but-off-subgroup input.\n   466\t- *In-circuit*, `from_coordinates_incircuit` routes to\n   467\t  `ForeignEdwardsEccChip::point_from_coordinates`\n   468\t  (`ecc/foreign/edwards_chip.rs:1087-1099`, confirmed unchanged at\n   469\t  circuits 7.2.4/`cfa6250`), which computes its witness hint as\n   470\t  `C::from_xy(x, y).map(|p| p.into_subgroup()).ok_or(...)`. The `ok_or`\n   471\t  only guards the off-curve case (`from_xy` returning `None`); an\n   472\t  on-curve, off-subgroup pair passes `from_xy` and then hits the same\n   473\t  panicking `into_subgroup()` **inside the `map`**, before the `ok_or`\n   474\t  can turn anything into an error. PR #485 (which fixed the decode half\n   475\t  above) left this call site alone — it kept the panicking\n   476\t  `into_subgroup()` as a default trait method on `CircuitCurve`\n   477\t  (`ecc/curves.rs:69-70`) specifically so call sites that haven't been\n   478\t  updated, like this one, keep compiling. **This half is still open.**\n   479\t\n   480\tThe *constraints* the chip emits are sound either way (the assigned\n   481\tpoint is forced into the subgroup by construction — same\n   482\tcofactor-clearing idiom as Jubjub's chip, see \"Also checked, found\n   483\tenforced\" below — so off-subgroup coordinates are unsatisfiable); the\n   484\tdefect is purely that the prover **panics while computing the hint**\n   485\tinstead of failing. In the standard prove flow this path is shielded by\n   486\tthe off-circuit arm: preprocess runs `from_coordinates_offcircuit` on\n   487\tthe same operands first and rejects gracefully, so synthesis with\n   488\tconcrete off-subgroup values is never reached (and keygen synthesizes\n   489\twith `Value::unknown()`, which never evaluates the hint closure). It is\n   490\tstill worth fixing together with the decode path — the panic is one\n   491\t`.expect(...)` shared by both, and the shielding is an artifact of the\n   492\tcurrent call order, not a checked invariant.\n   493\t\n   494\t**Reachability, checked directly.** Not live today: `zkir-v3` is not\n   495\tpart of the default/production `ledger` build (`ledger/Cargo.toml`:\n   496\t`default = [\"proof-verifying\"]`; `zkir_v3` is only pulled in via the\n   497\t`test-utilities` feature, explicitly commented *\"disables a small\n   498\tamount of well-formedness checking for test setups\"* — confirmed\n   499\tunchanged at this pin). Reaching final `3.0.0` (this pin — up from\n   500\t`3.0.0-rc.2`) removes one signal that this was pre-production, but the\n   501\tfeature-gating is the actual mechanism keeping it out of default\n   502\tbuilds, and that's unchanged. So the witness-hint panic can't be\n   503\ttriggered against anything live right now — but it would become live\n   504\tthe moment `zkir-v3`/Curve25519 ships into a production build (watch\n   505\tfor the crate leaving `test-utilities`-only status), and should be\n   506\tfixed before then rather than after.\n   507\t\n   508\t**Suggested action (witness-hint half only — decode is done).**\n   509\tReplace `from_coordinates_incircuit`'s witness hint with\n   510\t`try_into_subgroup` instead of `into_subgroup` — the exact one-line\n   511\tswap `from_public_input` already got in 7.2.4, applied to the one\n   512\tremaining call site. `midnight-ledger` PR #679 (finding 5's canonicity\n   513\tfix) doesn't cover this either: that branch predates Curve25519's\n   514\tmerge into the crate.\n   515\t\n   516\t## 12. EcMulGenerator: the one instruction not extended to the new curves\n   517\t\n   518\tLike finding 11, this was found by direct source reading while\n   519\tmechanizing Secp256r1/Curve25519 — not by the 1–10 sweep, which ran\n   520\tbefore either curve existed in the crate. Confirmed unchanged at this\n   521\tpin.\n   522\t\n   523\t**What the Rust does.** `EcMulGenerator` (`out = scalar · G`) supports\n   524\tonly `JubjubScalar` and `Secp256k1Scalar`. Both dispatch sites agree:\n   525\toff-circuit, anything else hits\n   526\t`bail!(\"Unsupported EcMulGenerator for scalar of type {t:?}\")`\n   527\t(`ir_vm.rs:559-568`); in-circuit, the same fall-through is an\n   528\t`Error::Synthesis` (`ir_vm.rs:1064-1083`). So `Secp256r1Scalar` and\n   529\t`Curve25519Scalar` operands are rejected on both sides.\n   530\t\n   531\t**Not a divergence — a parity gap, not a ZK-completeness issue.**\n   532\tOff-circuit and in-circuit agree, and our model matches both (no\n   533\tSecp256r1/Curve25519 arms), so there is no layer-2 finding here, and\n   534\tnothing is actually unbuildable: a producer wanting `scalar · G` on\n   535\tthe new curves today can always emit `Assign`(G) + `EcMul` instead, so\n   536\tthis isn't a missing capability the way findings 6/7/8's completeness\n   537\tgaps are (a source `preprocess` accepts that the circuit genuinely\n   538\tcannot build at all). It's about the Rust's own API surface being\n   539\tinconsistent: when Secp256r1 (#655) and Curve25519 (#658) were added,\n   540\t**every other type-dispatching instruction** (`Add`, `Mul`, `Neg`,\n   541\t`Inv`, `EcMul`, `TestEq`, `ConstrainEq`, `CondSelect`, the coordinate\n   542\tand byte conversions, `Encode`/`Assign`) gained arms for the new\n   543\ttypes — `EcMulGenerator` alone did not. There is no technical\n   544\tblocker: the two supported arms are just `assign_fixed(generator)` +\n   545\tthe shared `ec_mul_incircuit`, and both chip methods are generic over\n   546\tthe curve (`ForeignWeierstrassEccChip::assign_fixed` over any\n   547\t`WeierstrassCurve`; the foreign Edwards chip exposes `assign_fixed`\n   548\ttoo, and `ec_mul_incircuit` already dispatches Curve25519 for\n   549\t`EcMul`). The only real cost of leaving it as-is is efficiency, not\n   550\tcorrectness: a dedicated fixed-base-multiply gadget is typically\n   570\tinstructions — the code comment states the assumption directly: *\"We\n   571\tcan figure out if a type is used in the circuit by looking at the\n   572\tentry points, currently: PublicInput or PrivateInput.\"*\n   573\t\n   574\t**The entry point that assumption misses.** `FromBytes32` mints a\n   575\tfresh foreign-field value from a `Bytes32` (`val_t =\n   576\tSecp256r1Base/Scalar` etc.) — no foreign declared input or\n   577\ttranscript read required. (It is the *only* such fresh entry:\n   578\t`FromCoordinates` needs pre-existing foreign base-field operands.)\n   579\tIts in-circuit arm calls the gated chip accessor —\n   580\t`std_lib.p256()` (`from_bytes32.rs:117-127`) — and in\n   581\t`midnight-zk-stdlib 2.3.5` (the version this pin's `Cargo.lock`\n   582\tresolves for `zkir-v3`, up from `2.3.3`; unaffected by the version\n   583\tbump) that accessor is `panic!(\"ZkStdLibArch must enable p256\")` when\n   584\tthe flag is off (`lib.rs:779-784`, confirmed unchanged at `cfa6250`).\n   585\tEach link read directly at the stated versions.\n   586\t\n   587\t**The trigger is natural, not pathological.** A source whose only\n   588\tforeign-field values come from `FromBytes32` is the ordinary\n   589\thash-to-scalar pattern: take a `Bytes32`, convert to a foreign\n   590\tscalar, do arithmetic. Such a source is well-typed, passes the\n   591\tproducer obligations, and preprocesses fine off-circuit — then\n   592\t**panics the prover during circuit synthesis** (including at keygen:\n   593\tthe chip accessor is hit while building the circuit, before any\n   594\twitness values exist). The circuit cannot be constructed at all.\n   595\t\n   596\t**Severity and reachability.** Prover-side robustness/completeness\n   597\t(same class as findings 10 and 11): no soundness impact, and not live\n   598\ttoday for the same reason as finding 11 (`zkir-v3` is gated behind the\n   599\t`test-utilities` feature, not in the default `ledger` build — confirmed\n   600\tunchanged at this pin, despite `zkir-v3` itself now being a final\n   601\t`3.0.0` rather than a release candidate). Needs fixing before\n   602\t`zkir-v3` ships.\n   603\t\n   604\t**Model-scope note.** Our mechanization has no `ZkStdLibArch` layer,\n   605\tso for this source class the model deems the circuit satisfiable\n   606\twhile the deployed prover cannot build it — a documented modeling\n   607\tboundary (the arch/chip-selection layer sits below the constraint\n   608\tcontracts the model describes), not a model bug; the WF layer does\n   609\tnot, and per the above cannot by type-inspection alone, exclude the\n   610\tclass.\n   611\t\n   612\t**Suggested action.** Extend `used_chips` to also inspect\n   613\t`FromBytes32`'s `val_t` (a one-arm addition to the existing\n   614\tinstruction scan it already performs for the hash chips), or make the\n   615\tstdlib accessors return an error instead of panicking. The former is\n   616\tthe targeted fix; the comment's \"entry points\" list simply needs\n   617\t`FromBytes32` added to it.\n   618\t\n   619\t## Open questions from the 2026-08-05 conformance-reading pass — now resolved\n   620\t\n   621\tFindings 6–10 above came from a full per-instruction, three-axis\n   622\tlayer-2 conformance-reading pass (semantics / circuit / encoding)\n   623\tcovering all 34 instructions at this same pin — extending findings\n   624\t1–5, which came from an earlier, partial hand-review. That pass\n    30\t        K: synthErr: ec_mul_generator output: Unsupported EcMulGenerator for scalar of type Secp256r1Scalar | Rust: Unsupported EcMulGenerator for scalar of type Secp256r1Scalar\n    31\tPASS  f13_chip_gating_from_bytes32               Finding 13           K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=from_bytes32:synthErr\n    32\t        the secp256k1 chip is never initialised (used_chips ignores from_bytes32)\n    33\t        K: synthErr: chip not initialised for Secp256k1Base | Rust: ok\n    34\tPASS  k01_jubjub_from_coordinates_parity_only    K1 (new)             K=ok       Rust=ok     gate=from_coordinates:violated\n    35\t        off-circuit only the parity of x is used (decompression), so a wrong x yields the real point; in-circuit (x, y) must lie on the curve\n    36\t        K: violated: from_coordinates: (x, y) is not on the curve | Rust: ok\n    37\tPASS  f01_reconstitute_overflow                  Finding 1 (retired)  K=error    Rust=error  gate=reconstitute_field:unknown\n    38\t        divisor * 2^8 + modulus >= r: off-circuit overflow error; in circuit the sum would wrap mod r\n    39\t        K: unknown: register %o is not in the witness | Rust: Reconstituted element overflows field\n    40\t\n    41\t13/13 divergence cases behave as expected\nevidence/zkir-k-unit-values-2026-09-05.txt\nevidence/zkir-k-unit-hash-2026-09-05.txt\nevidence/zkir-k-divergence-tests-2026-09-05.txt\nevidence/k-rust-compatibility-2026-09-05.txt\nevidence/zkir-k-differential-ext-2ffe2d1-2026-09-05.txt\nevidence/zkir-k-differential-92e8bdd3-2026-09-05.txt\nevidence/zkir-k-milestone2-corpus-check-2026-09-05.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"agent_message","text":"The `uv` check could not start because it needs a lock file in the read-only cache. I’ll keep the runtime claims limited to the existing receipts and label new counterexamples as source-derived. Source tracing also shows that K1’s program never enables the Jubjub chip, and that `less_than` at widths 253 and 254 reaches a chip assertion that K omits."}}
